const QUESTIONS = [{"n": 1, "image": null, "stem": "A 1-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department because of a 3-day history of low-grade fever and watery diarrhea. She has had 8 to 10 watery stools daily that contain mucus but no blood; she has had fewer wet diapers than usual. She has not had vomiting or symptoms of an upper respiratory tract infection, but her father reports that she became very fussy earlier today. She has been drinking juice and children's electrolyte supplement but not eating much food. Her older brother had a mild upper respiratory tract infection and diarrhea last week. On arrival, she appears lethargic. She is at the 25th percentile for length and weight. Her temperature is 37.5°C (99.5°F), pulse is 160/min, respirations are 30/min, and blood pressure is 70/40 mm Hg. The mucous membranes are dry. The lungs are clear to auscultation. Cardiac examination shows a grade 1/6, systolic ejection murmur with no rub or gallop. The abdomen is soft, and there is no hepatosplenomegaly. The extremities are cool, and capillary refill time is 3 to 4 seconds. The remainder of the examination shows no abnormalities. Laboratory studies show:", "labs": [{"name": "Serum", "rows": [["Na⁺", "134 mEq/L"], ["K⁺", "3.2 mEq/L"], ["Cl⁻", "105 mEq/L"], ["HCO₃⁻", "8 mEq/L"], ["Urea nitrogen", "25 mg/dL"], ["Glucose", "88 mg/dL"], ["Creatinine", "0.5 mg/dL"]]}, {"name": "Urine", "rows": [["pH", "5"], ["Specific gravity", "1.025"], ["Glucose", "negative"], ["Protein", "negative"], ["Ketones", "3+"], ["Gram stain", "no organisms"]]}], "stemTail": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [["A", "Celiac disease"], ["B", "Diabetic ketoacidosis"], ["C", "Inflammatory bowel disease"], ["D", "Lactic acidosis"], ["E", "Renal tubular acidosis"]]},
{"n": 2, "image": null, "stem": "A 3-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department because of a 2-day history of watery diarrhea and vomiting. During this period, he has not had an appetite for food but has been drinking sugar-free soda. There has not been any change in urinary output. On arrival, he is alert and interactive. His temperature is 37.5°C (99.5°F), pulse is 120/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 90/60 mm Hg. Examination shows a soft, nontender abdomen and no organomegaly. Bowel sounds are normal. Serum studies show:", "labs": [{"rows": [["Na⁺", "140 mEq/L"], ["K⁺", "4.5 mEq/L"], ["Cl⁻", "110 mEq/L"], ["HCO₃⁻", "15 mEq/L"], ["Glucose", "45 mg/dL"]]}], "stemTail": "Which of the following is the most likely cause of these findings?", "options": [["A", "Adrenal insufficiency"], ["B", "Fatty acid oxidation defect"], ["C", "Fructose 1-phosphate aldolase deficiency"], ["D", "Inadequate caloric intake"], ["E", "Insulin deficiency"]]},
{"n": 3, "image": null, "stem": "A 10-day-old male newborn is brought to the emergency department because of poor feeding and drowsiness for the past day. He also has had two episodes of vomiting today. He was born at term following an uncomplicated pregnancy and delivery and weighed 3600 g (7 lb 15 oz). On arrival, he is lethargic. His skin color is a shade darker than his parents' skin color. His temperature is 36.1°C (97°F), pulse is 180/min, respirations are 70/min, and blood pressure is 58/30 mm Hg. The anterior fontanel is sunken, and the scalp has sparse fine blond hairs. The nipples and scrotum are darkly pigmented. The pupils are equal, round, and reactive to light and accommodation. There is no scleral icterus. The lungs are clear to auscultation. Cardiac examination shows a regular rhythm and no murmurs. S1 and S2 are normal. The abdomen is soft with no masses or organomegaly. Genital development is Tanner stage 1; the penis is 4 cm long. Testes are descended. Serum studies are most likely to show which of the following?", "choiceHead": ["Na⁺", "K⁺", "Glucose"], "options": [["A", "Decreased · decreased · decreased"], ["B", "Decreased · increased · decreased"], ["C", "Increased · decreased · increased"], ["D", "Increased · increased · increased"], ["E", "Normal · decreased · increased"], ["F", "Normal · increased · decreased"]]},
{"n": 4, "image": null, "stem": "A 2½-year-old boy is brought to the physician by his mother because she is concerned about his language development. He uses fewer words than his two older siblings did at the same age. He was born at term following an uncomplicated pregnancy and delivery. He has no history of serious illness and takes no medications. He began walking at the age of 11 months. He feeds himself, plays well with his siblings, and does well in day care. Motor development is appropriate for age. He knows 100 words but does not use them in phrases. Examination shows no abnormalities. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in evaluating this child's language development?", "options": [["A", "Intelligence testing"], ["B", "Speech evaluation"], ["C", "Audiometry"], ["D", "MRI of the inner ears"], ["E", "No further evaluation is indicated at this time"]]},
{"n": 5, "image": null, "stem": "A previously healthy 17-year-old girl comes to the physician 4 days after her sexual partner was diagnosed with gonorrhea. Examination shows no abnormalities. Tests for Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis are ordered. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [["A", "Intramuscular ceftriaxone therapy only"], ["B", "Intramuscular ceftriaxone and oral doxycycline"], ["C", "Oral azithromycin therapy only"], ["D", "Oral azithromycin and intramuscular penicillin therapy"], ["E", "No treatment indicated because the patient is asymptomatic"]]},
{"n": 6, "image": null, "stem": "A previously healthy 9-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of moderate groin pain since he participated in a hockey game 24 hours ago. He recalls no trauma or injury to this area. He reports a decreased appetite today. He has not had fever. He appears well. Vital signs are within normal limits. Examination shows edema of the left aspect of the scrotum. There is marked tenderness to palpation of the superior aspect of the left testicle; the pain does not improve with elevation of the testicle. A 3-mm area of bluish discoloration overlays the point of maximal tenderness; there is no erythema. Cremasteric reflex is intact. Genital and pubic hair development are Tanner stage 2. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [["A", "Epididymitis"], ["B", "Incarcerated hernia"], ["C", "Orchitis"], ["D", "Testicular hematoma"], ["E", "Torsion of the appendix testis"]]},
{"n": 7, "image": null, "stem": "A 1-year-old boy is brought to the physician for a follow-up examination. He completed a 1-week course of intravenous ceftriaxone followed by a 5-day course of amoxicillin for pneumococcal pneumonia 2 weeks ago and has been asymptomatic since then. He has had six episodes of acute otitis media during the past 6 months. Immunizations are up-to-date. He is at the 50th percentile for length and weight. Tympanic membranes are dull with clear effusions. The remainder of the examination shows no abnormalities. Laboratory studies show:", "labs": [{"rows": [["Hemoglobin", "12 g/dL"], ["Leukocyte count", "8500/mm³"], ["  Segmented neutrophils", "40%"], ["  Lymphocytes", "50%"], ["  Monocytes", "10%"], ["Platelet count", "420,000/mm³"]]}, {"name": "Serum", "rows": [["IgA", "10 mg/dL"], ["IgE", "10 IU/mL"], ["IgG", "100 mg/dL"], ["IgM", "20 mg/dL"]]}], "stemTail": "Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment of this patient's underlying condition?", "options": [["A", "Amoxicillin prophylaxis"], ["B", "Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor therapy"], ["C", "Oral prednisone therapy"], ["D", "Monthly infusion of immune globulin"], ["E", "Splenectomy"]]},
{"n": 8, "image": null, "stem": "A 2-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department 30 minutes after he was found in his grandmother's bedroom, surrounded by multiple open medication bottles. His grandmother states that her medications include imipramine, phenobarbital, iron supplements, aspirin, and oxycodone. On arrival, the patient is somnolent. His temperature is 36°C (96.8°F), pulse is 70/min, respirations are 10/min, and blood pressure is 60/40 mm Hg. The pupils are 2 mm and nonreactive to light. The lungs are clear to auscultation. He is unresponsive to painful stimuli. Ingestion of which of the following is the most likely cause of these findings?", "options": [["A", "Aspirin"], ["B", "Imipramine"], ["C", "Iron"], ["D", "Oxycodone"], ["E", "Phenobarbital"]]},
{"n": 9, "image": null, "stem": "A 6-year-old girl is brought to the physician by her father because of a 36-hour history of fever and a 1-day history of pain and swelling of her joints. The pain and swelling began in her right knee, then increased to involve both knees, and has now migrated to her left ankle and left elbow. She is currently unable to walk because of the pain. Two weeks ago, she had a sore throat that resolved after 2 days without treatment. She has no known drug allergies. She appears uncomfortable when she moves. She is at the 50th percentile for height and weight. Her temperature is 39°C (102.2°F), pulse is 136/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 100/60 mm Hg. Examination shows no rash. There is a grade 2/6, pansystolic, high-pitched murmur that is heard best at the apex and accentuated when she makes a fist. There is mild swelling of the left elbow and both knees; the left elbow and left knee are warm. Her erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 110 mm/h. An antistreptolysin O titer is 800 Todd/U (N<240). Salicylate therapy is begun and results in marked improvement of the patient's symptoms. Which of the following is the most appropriate pharmacotherapy to prevent a recurrence of this patient's disorder?", "options": [["A", "Clindamycin"], ["B", "Erythromycin"], ["C", "Penicillin"], ["D", "Prednisone"], ["E", "No pharmacotherapy is indicated"]]},
{"n": 10, "image": null, "stem": "A 3977-g (8-lb 12-oz) male newborn is delivered at term to a 30-year-old primigravid woman. Apgar scores are 8 and 9 at 1 and 5 minutes, respectively. The mother's hepatitis B status is unknown because she received no prenatal care. Maternal hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg) assay is ordered. Examination of the newborn shows no abnormalities. In addition to administration of hepatitis B vaccine (HBV) now, which of the following is the most appropriate treatment regimen for the newborn?", "options": [["A", "Observation only"], ["B", "Hepatitis B immune globulin (HBIG) only now"], ["C", "HBIG at 2 and 6 months of age"], ["D", "HBV at 2 and 6 months of age"], ["E", "HBIG only if maternal HBsAg is positive and HBV at 2 and 6 months of age"]]},
{"n": 11, "image": null, "stem": "A 3-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department because of a 5-day history of increased thirst and urination and a 2-day history of decreased activity. Although she had been previously toilet trained and dry at night, she has had several episodes of nocturnal wetting over the past week. She has not had fever, vomiting, or diarrhea. Two weeks ago, she fell off her tricycle, but no injuries were apparent. Her temperature is 37.5°C (99.5°F), pulse is 160/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 80/60 mm Hg. Examination shows sunken eyes and dry mucous membranes. The remainder of the examination shows no abnormalities. Laboratory studies show:", "labs": [{"name": "Serum", "rows": [["Na⁺", "159 mEq/L"], ["Cl⁻", "125 mEq/L"], ["K⁺", "4.5 mEq/L"], ["HCO₃⁻", "15 mEq/L"], ["Urea nitrogen", "43 mg/dL"], ["Glucose", "122 mg/dL"], ["Creatinine", "1.0 mg/dL"]]}, {"name": "Urine", "rows": [["pH", "6"], ["Specific gravity", "1.001"], ["Blood", "negative"], ["Glucose", "negative"], ["Protein", "negative"], ["Osmolality", "125 mOsmol/kg"]]}], "stemTail": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [["A", "Chronic renal failure"], ["B", "Diabetes insipidus"], ["C", "Diabetes mellitus"], ["D", "Inappropriate secretion of ADH (vasopressin)"], ["E", "Psychogenic polydipsia"], ["F", "Urinary tract infection"]]},
{"n": 12, "image": "q12", "stem": "A 1-month-old girl is brought to the emergency department because of increasing irritability and difficulty breathing over the past 6 hours. She has a 3-day history of nasal congestion, cough, and rectal temperatures to 37.5°C (99.5°F). Over the past 2 days, she has been given an over-the-counter cough and cold preparation every 4 hours without improvement, and her parents have been suctioning her nose after applying saline nose drops. She has only breast-fed once in the past 6 hours. Her rectal temperature is 37.3°C (99.1°F), pulse is 260/min, respirations are 65/min, and blood pressure is 102/68 mm Hg. The lungs are clear to auscultation. The liver edge is palpated 2 cm below the right costal margin, and the spleen is not palpable. An ECG is shown. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [["A", "Atrial fibrillation"], ["B", "Long QT interval syndrome"], ["C", "Sinus tachycardia"], ["D", "Supraventricular tachycardia"], ["E", "Ventricular tachycardia"]]},
{"n": 13, "image": "q13", "stem": "A previously healthy 14-month-old girl is brought to the physician by her mother because of a 2-month history of the left eye wandering. She was born at term following an uncomplicated pregnancy and delivery. Examination of the eye shows left eye wandering. A photograph of the eyes is shown. The remainder of the physical examination shows no abnormalities. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [["A", "Acquired exotropia"], ["B", "Chronic endophthalmitis"], ["C", "Congenital glaucoma"], ["D", "Familial cataract"], ["E", "Retinoblastoma"]]},
{"n": 14, "image": null, "stem": "A 10-year-old boy develops a rash on the chest 2 days after returning from a beach vacation. Over the next 2 weeks, the lesions spread to the back, abdomen, and extremities. There are multiple well-demarcated papules and small, thin, round plaques covered with fine white scales over the torso and extremities. Removal of a scale causes minor bleeding at the site. There is pitting of the fingernails. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [["A", "Cellulitis"], ["B", "Nummular eczema"], ["C", "Pityriasis rosea"], ["D", "Psoriasis"], ["E", "Tinea corporis"]]},
{"n": 15, "image": null, "stem": "A previously healthy 13-month-old boy is brought to the emergency department 1 hour after the acute onset of mild difficulty breathing and a persistent, nonproductive cough. He was born at term following an uncomplicated pregnancy, labor, and delivery. Immunizations are up-to-date. On arrival, he is alert but in mild respiratory distress. His temperature is 37°C (98.6°F), pulse is 110/min, respirations are 36/min, and blood pressure is 85/50 mm Hg. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 97%. Examination shows no nasal discharge or stridor. Expiratory wheezes are heard bilaterally, but they are greater on the right. His symptoms do not improve following treatment with nebulized albuterol. A chest x-ray shows hyperinflation of the right lung but no infiltrate or foreign body. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [["A", "Chest percussion and physiotherapy"], ["B", "Oral administration of amoxicillin"], ["C", "Oral administration of corticosteroids"], ["D", "Subcutaneous administration of epinephrine"], ["E", "Endoscopic examination of the patient's airway"]]},
{"n": 16, "image": null, "stem": "A 5-year-old boy has had pain in the groin and thigh for 2 weeks; 1 week ago, he began limping. There is no history of recent febrile illness. X-rays of the hips show normal findings. A bone scan shows decreased uptake in the right hip. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [["A", "Ankylosing spondylitis"], ["B", "Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease"], ["C", "Pauciarticular juvenile rheumatoid arthritis"], ["D", "Septic arthritis"], ["E", "Slipped capital femoral epiphysis"], ["F", "Systemic lupus erythematosus"], ["G", "Toxic synovitis"]]},
{"n": 17, "image": null, "stem": "A 3-month-old girl is brought to the emergency department by her parents because of a 1-day history of fever and poor feeding. She vomited once 6 hours ago, and her parents have noticed several lesions on her skin since then. They say she has been sleepy and has not been acting like herself. She has not had diarrhea. She was born after an uncomplicated pregnancy and delivery. She is at the 25th percentile for length, weight, and head circumference. Her temperature is 38°C (100.4°F), pulse is 180/min, respirations are 40/min, and blood pressure is 63/45 mm Hg. Examination shows 3- to 8-mm, nonblanching, erythematous lesions over the scalp, trunk, and proximal upper and lower extremities. The remainder of the examination shows no abnormalities. Serum studies show:", "labs": [{"rows": [["Na⁺", "136 mEq/L"], ["K⁺", "3.6 mEq/L"], ["Cl⁻", "100 mEq/L"], ["HCO₃⁻", "17 mEq/L"]]}, {"intro": "Arterial blood gas analysis on room air shows:", "rows": [["pH", "7.32"], ["PCO2", "38 mm Hg"], ["PO2", "94 mm Hg"]]}], "stemTail": "In addition to administration of intravenous antibiotics, which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [["A", "Administration of dopamine"], ["B", "Administration of epinephrine"], ["C", "Administration of 0.9% saline"], ["D", "Administration of sodium bicarbonate"], ["E", "Endotracheal intubation"]]},
{"n": 18, "image": null, "stem": "A 4-year-old African American girl has pallor and progressive malaise 6 weeks after being diagnosed with severe hepatitis A. Examination 4 weeks after diagnosis showed improvement in symptoms. She now has scattered petechiae and ecchymoses. Laboratory studies show:", "labs": [{"rows": [["Hemoglobin", "6.5 g/dL"], ["Mean corpuscular volume", "82 µm³"], ["Leukocyte count", "2100/mm³"], ["Reticulocyte count", "1.0%"], ["Platelet count", "21,000/mm³"], ["Serum iron", "250 µg/dL"]]}], "stemTail": "A blood smear shows normochromic, normocytic cells. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [["A", "Anemia, acute hemorrhagic"], ["B", "Anemia, aplastic"], ["C", "Anemia, autoimmune hemolytic"], ["D", "Anemia, chronic disease"], ["E", "Glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency"], ["F", "Hereditary spherocytosis"], ["G", "Sickle cell disease"], ["H", "Sickle cell trait"], ["I", "Thalassemia minor"]]},
{"n": 19, "image": null, "stem": "A previously healthy 17-year-old girl comes to the physician for a follow-up examination after a PPD skin test produced 17 mm of induration at 48 hours. She takes no medications. She drinks alcohol and smokes cigarettes and marijuana occasionally. She is sexually active and uses condoms inconsistently for contraception. She lives in a rural community, and she raises chickens and cows. She participates in the student government and volunteers at a skilled nursing care facility. She traveled to Ireland for 10 days last summer with her high school. Examination shows no additional abnormalities. Which of the following activities is the strongest predisposing factor for this patient's positive PPD skin test?", "options": [["A", "Overseas travel"], ["B", "Participation in high school events"], ["C", "Raising livestock"], ["D", "Sexual activity"], ["E", "Smoking marijuana"], ["F", "Volunteering at a skilled nursing care facility"]]},
{"n": 20, "image": null, "stem": "A 7-year-old girl is brought for a follow-up examination 2 days after completing a 10-day course of antibiotics for her second episode of pneumococcal pneumonia during the past year; during this time, she has also had two episodes of otitis media and one episode of herpes zoster. She was born at term following an uncomplicated pregnancy and delivery and was healthy until 1 year ago when she began to have frequent upper and lower respiratory tract infections. Her mother was recently hospitalized for treatment of nontuberculous mycobacterial bacteremia. Examination of the patient shows 2-cm nontender lymph nodes in the axillae. There is fluid behind both tympanic membranes. The remainder of the examination shows no abnormalities. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [["A", "Chronic granulomatous disease"], ["B", "HIV infection"], ["C", "Hyperimmunoglobulinemia E syndrome"], ["D", "Thymic-parathyroid dysplasia (DiGeorge syndrome)"], ["E", "X-linked agammaglobulinemia"]]},
{"n": 21, "image": null, "stem": "A previously healthy 8-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department because of fever, abdominal pain, and bloody diarrhea for 6 hours. He and his family recently immigrated to the USA from Mexico. Abdominal examination shows pain and tenderness in the right upper quadrant. There is mild hepatomegaly. Sigmoidoscopy with mucosal biopsy shows flask-shaped ulcers with healthy intervening mucosa. Which of the following is the most likely pathogen?", "options": [["A", "Entamoeba histolytica"], ["B", "Giardia lamblia"], ["C", "Rotavirus"], ["D", "Salmonella typhi"], ["E", "Shigella sonnei"]]},
{"n": 22, "image": null, "stem": "A 2-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department 30 minutes after he fell approximately 8 feet from a backyard porch onto a firm dirt surface, landing on his back. On arrival, he appears mildly uncomfortable. His temperature is 37°C (98.6°F), pulse is 140/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 85/55 mm Hg. Examination shows moderate right-sided flank tenderness. The most appropriate next step in diagnosis is measurement of which of the following?", "options": [["A", "Serum creatinine"], ["B", "Serum urea nitrogen"], ["C", "Urine blood"], ["D", "Urine protein"], ["E", "Urine volume"]]},
{"n": 23, "image": null, "stem": "A 4-month-old girl is admitted to the hospital because of severe coughing for 2 days. The cough is episodic and staccato-like and often followed by vomiting. One week ago, she developed rhinorrhea. On admission, she appears well when not coughing. Her temperature is 37°C (98.6°F), pulse is 120/min, and respirations are 34/min. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 95%. Examination shows a few petechiae on the face and thick nasal secretions. An x-ray of the chest shows no abnormalities. Which of the following is the most appropriate intervention to prevent nosocomial transmission?", "options": [["A", "Antiviral therapy for all health care workers"], ["B", "Contact isolation"], ["C", "Droplet isolation"], ["D", "Erythromycin therapy for all health care workers"], ["E", "Intravenous immune globulin therapy for all health care workers"], ["F", "Intravenous immune globulin therapy for the patient"], ["G", "Pertussis vaccination for all health care workers"], ["H", "Pertussis vaccination for the patient"], ["I", "No intervention"]]},
{"n": 24, "image": null, "stem": "An 11-year-old girl with spina bifida is brought to the physician because of right thigh pain for 2 days. She is paraplegic and has limited sensation below the waist. Her temperature is 37°C (98.6°F), pulse is 95/min, and respirations are 16/min. Examination shows swelling of the right distal thigh without redness. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": [["A", "Reassurance"], ["B", "Application of local heat"], ["C", "X-ray of the lower extremity"], ["D", "Range of motion exercises"], ["E", "Acetaminophen therapy"]]},
{"n": 25, "image": null, "stem": "A 16-year-old girl is brought to the physician because she has not had a menstrual period for 4 months. Menarche was at the age of 12 years; menses previously occurred at regular 30-day intervals. She also reports increased hair growth between her breasts and on her chin as well as acne on her forehead and nose. She had sexual intercourse for the first time at the age of 14 years; she most recently had sexual intercourse 5 months ago. She and her partners have used condoms consistently. She is 163 cm (5 ft 4 in) tall and weighs 84 kg (185 lb); BMI is 32 kg/m². Physical examination shows coarse hair over the chin and dark, velvety skin over the back of the neck. There are numerous papules and pustules over the face. The abdomen is obese, soft, and nontender; there are no masses. Breast and pubic hair development is Tanner stage 5. A pregnancy test is negative. Which of the following is the most likely location of the pathology causing this patient's symptoms?", "options": [["A", "Hypothalamus"], ["B", "Ovary"], ["C", "Pancreas"], ["D", "Pituitary gland"], ["E", "Thyroid gland"]]},
{"n": 26, "image": null, "stem": "An asymptomatic 15-year-old boy is brought to the physician for a well-child examination. He is concerned because he is the shortest student in his class. Review of his records from the age of 8 years to today shows a consistent pattern of height at the 3rd percentile and weight at the 25th percentile. He has no history of serious illness and takes no medications. His last routine examination 2 years ago showed no abnormalities. His mother is 165 cm (5 ft 5 in) tall, and his father is 178 cm (5 ft 10 in) tall. He says his parents are always nagging him to eat better, but he thinks his diet is okay. Genital and pubic hair development is Tanner stage 2. The remainder of the examination shows no abnormalities. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's short stature?", "options": [["A", "Adrenal insufficiency"], ["B", "Constitutional growth delay"], ["C", "Familial short stature"], ["D", "Growth hormone deficiency"], ["E", "Insufficient caloric intake"]]},
{"n": 27, "image": null, "stem": "A 5-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of clumsiness and frequent falls over the past 6 months. He did not walk independently until 17 months of age and has always had poor motor skills. There is enlargement of the calf muscles and a positive Gowers sign. Which of the following is the most likely inheritance pattern?", "options": [["A", "Autosomal dominant"], ["B", "Autosomal recessive"], ["C", "X-linked dominant"], ["D", "X-linked recessive"], ["E", "No known mendelian pattern"]]},
{"n": 28, "image": null, "stem": "A 1-month-old boy is brought for a well-child examination. He was born at term following an uncomplicated pregnancy. Fifteen minutes after delivery, a very soft systolic murmur was heard along the left sternal border. He appears well today. His temperature is 37°C (98.6°F), pulse is 140/min, respirations are 28/min, and blood pressure is 76/54 mm Hg. He is at the 50th percentile for length and weight. A grade 3/6 holosystolic murmur is heard best at the middle left sternal border. Examination shows no other abnormalities. Which of the following is the most likely physiologic mechanism for the change in intensity of this patient's murmur?", "options": [["A", "Closed ductus arteriosus"], ["B", "Decreased pulmonary vascular resistance"], ["C", "Improved atrial natriuretic factor functioning"], ["D", "Improved myocardial contractility"], ["E", "Increased circulating blood volume"]]},
{"n": 29, "image": null, "stem": "A 3-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of eye swelling for 1 week. His blood pressure is 120/60 mm Hg. Examination shows generalized edema and ascites. Serum studies show a creatinine concentration of 0.4 mg/dL, albumin concentration of 1.4 g/dL, and cholesterol concentration of 569 mg/dL. Urinalysis shows 4+ protein and no blood. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [["A", "Acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis"], ["B", "Hemolytic-uremic syndrome"], ["C", "Henoch-Schonlein purpura with nephritis"], ["D", "Minimal change nephrotic syndrome"], ["E", "Nephrotic syndrome due to focal and segmental glomerulosclerosis"]]},
{"n": 30, "image": null, "stem": "A 2-year-old child with sickle cell hemoglobinopathy has a 2-week history of a paroxysmal cough followed by vomiting. Laboratory studies show:", "labs": [{"rows": [["Leukocyte count", "32,000/mm³"], ["  Segmented neutrophils", "10%"], ["  Lymphocytes", "85%"], ["  Monocytes", "5%"]]}], "stemTail": "An x-ray of the chest shows perihilar infiltrates. Which of the following vaccines could have prevented this condition?", "options": [["A", "Diphtheria-tetanus-acellular pertussis"], ["B", "Haemophilus influenzae type b-protein conjugate"], ["C", "Influenza virus"], ["D", "Measles-mumps-rubella"], ["E", "Pneumococcal"]]},
{"n": 31, "image": "q31", "stem": "A 5-month-old girl is brought to the physician because of daily episodes of vomiting after feedings. Her mother reports that the episodes began shortly after birth and that the vomiting is occasionally bilious. Her diet consists of soy-based formula; trials of multiple infant formulas have not stopped the vomiting. Abdominal examination shows no distention or tenderness. There are no masses. Normal bowel sounds are heard. An upper gastrointestinal series is shown. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [["A", "Cyclic vomiting syndrome"], ["B", "Gastroesophageal reflux disease"], ["C", "Hiatal hernia"], ["D", "Intestinal malrotation"], ["E", "Pyloric stenosis"]]},
{"n": 32, "image": null, "stem": "A 13-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of a 2-week history of mild neck tenderness. She has not had fever, fatigue, sensitivity to hot or cold, or changes in weight. She has no history of serious illness and takes no medications. Her mother has systemic lupus erythematosus. The patient is at the 50th percentile for height and weight. Her pulse is 88/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 112/72 mm Hg. Examination shows normal skin texture. There is no exophthalmos. The neck is supple; there is no lymphadenopathy. The thyroid gland is diffusely enlarged; no nodules are palpated. Serum studies show:", "labs": [{"rows": [["Thyroid-stimulating hormone", "3.9 µU/mL"], ["Thyroxine (T4)", "10 µg/dL"], ["Triiodothyronine (T3)", "135 ng/dL"]]}], "stemTail": "The patient's serum thyroid antiperoxidase antibody concentration is 1000 IU/mL (N<20). Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [["A", "Acute suppurative thyroiditis"], ["B", "Chronic lymphocytic (Hashimoto) thyroiditis"], ["C", "Endemic goiter"], ["D", "Graves disease"], ["E", "Thyroid carcinoma"]]},
{"n": 33, "image": null, "stem": "A 14-year-old boy with a cardiac murmur is brought to the physician for a school physical examination. He reports that during this past soccer season, he easily became short of breath at times but did not seek medical attention. He has no symptoms of chest pain, palpitations, or syncope, and he appears healthy. His pulse is 72/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 126/42 mm Hg. Cardiac examination shows a blowing diastolic murmur at the upper right sternal border; there is no systolic murmur. Radial and pedal pulses are 3+ bilaterally. Which of the following is the most likely cause of these findings?", "options": [["A", "Increased pulmonary vascular resistance"], ["B", "Increased systemic cardiac output"], ["C", "Increased systemic vascular resistance"], ["D", "Left ventricular pressure overload"], ["E", "Left ventricular volume overload"]]},
{"n": 34, "image": null, "stem": "A 15-month-old boy is brought to the physician for an initial examination. According to his mother and previous medical records, he has been healthy, and developmental milestones are appropriate for age. At his last examination 3 months ago, he was at the 25th percentile for length and weight. Today, he is at the 25th percentile for length and the 10th percentile for weight. Examination shows no abnormalities. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in evaluation of his poor weight gain?", "options": [["A", "Recommend a diet diary with calorie counts"], ["B", "Reevaluate weight in 1 week"], ["C", "Test the stool for reducing substances and fecal fat"], ["D", "Admit to the hospital for observation"], ["E", "Obtain an x-ray of the abdomen"]]},
{"n": 35, "image": null, "stem": "A 10-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of swelling of the eyelids and feet for 3 days. Over the past week, she has had decreased urinary frequency. Two weeks ago, she had a sore throat that resolved spontaneously. Her temperature is 37.3°C (99.1°F), pulse is 100/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 140/85 mm Hg. Examination shows periorbital, pretibial, and pedal edema. Urinalysis shows:", "labs": [{"rows": [["pH", "6"], ["Specific gravity", "1.015"], ["Blood", "large"], ["Protein", "3+"], ["RBC", "too numerous to count"], ["WBC", "5-10/hpf"], ["RBC casts", "10-20/hpf"]]}], "stemTail": "Which of the following is the most likely cause of the hypertension?", "options": [["A", "Catecholamine excess"], ["B", "Idiopathic hypertension"], ["C", "Mineralocorticoid excess"], ["D", "Stimulation of the parasympathetic system"], ["E", "Volume overload"]]},
{"n": 36, "image": null, "stem": "A 3-day-old girl with Down syndrome has not had a bowel movement since birth. She feeds poorly but has not vomited. She has not had any other symptoms. She was born at term to a 39-year-old primigravid woman following an uncomplicated pregnancy and spontaneous vaginal delivery. Rupture of the membranes occurred 6 hours prior to delivery; there was no meconium staining of fluid. Apgar scores were 6 and 9 at 1 and 5 minutes, respectively. The patient's temperature is 37.8°C (100°F), pulse is 140/min, respirations are 40/min, and blood pressure is 90/55 mm Hg. Examination shows epicanthal folds, white stellate spots on the irides, single palmar creases, and wide spaces between the great and second toes. The abdomen is soft and nontender; there is no organomegaly. Muscle tone is decreased in the trunk. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for this patient's failure to pass stool?", "options": [["A", "Absence of ganglion cells in the bowel wall"], ["B", "Dilation of lymphatic vessels of the bowel"], ["C", "Duodenal atresia"], ["D", "Pancreatic enzyme insufficiency"], ["E", "Villous atrophy"]]},
{"n": 37, "image": null, "stem": "A 3-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of increasing lethargy and jaundice over the past week. At birth, he had jaundice treated with phototherapy for 5 days. His father had a splenectomy at the age of 6 years. Developmental milestones and diet are normal for age. Examination shows mild scleral icterus. A grade 2/6, systolic ejection murmur is heard. The spleen tip is palpated 4 cm below the left costal margin. Laboratory studies show:", "labs": [{"rows": [["Hemoglobin", "8.3 g/dL"], ["Leukocyte count", "6500/mm³"], ["  Segmented neutrophils", "40%"], ["  Lymphocytes", "55%"], ["  Monocytes", "5%"], ["Reticulocyte count", "10%"], ["Serum total bilirubin", "5 mg/dL"]]}], "stemTail": "Direct and indirect antiglobulin (Coombs) tests are negative. A blood smear is most likely to show which of the following types of erythrocytes?", "options": [["A", "Macrocytic and hyperchromic"], ["B", "Macrocytic and hypochromic"], ["C", "Microcytic and hypochromic"], ["D", "Normocytic and hypochromic"], ["E", "Normocytic and normochromic"]]},
{"n": 38, "image": null, "stem": "A 1-day-old newborn is jittery and irritable. He was born at term to a 25-year-old primigravid woman who received irregular prenatal care; maternal serology screening on admission was negative for hepatitis B, rubella, HIV, and syphilis. The newborn weighs 4706 g (10 lb 6 oz) and is pink. The anterior fontanelle is 1.5 cm, and the posterior fontanelle is barely patent. The lungs are clear to auscultation. Cardiac examination shows a regular rate and rhythm with a grade 2/6 holosystolic murmur throughout the precordium and strong femoral pulses bilaterally. The abdomen is soft without masses or hepatosplenomegaly. Genital examination shows no abnormalities. Neurologic examination shows slight jitteriness but normal tone. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the newborn's neurologic findings?", "options": [["A", "Adrenal insufficiency"], ["B", "Hypermagnesemia"], ["C", "Hypocalcemia"], ["D", "Hypoglycemia"], ["E", "Hypothyroidism"]]},
{"n": 39, "image": null, "stem": "Twelve hours after undergoing circumcision, a 3-day-old boy has persistent bleeding at his circumcision site. He was born at 38 weeks' gestation to a 23-year-old primigravid woman following an uncomplicated pregnancy and delivery. He was given an intramuscular injection of vitamin K immediately after delivery. His temperature is 37.5°C (99.5°F), pulse is 140/min, respirations are 55/min, and blood pressure is 60/40 mm Hg. Examination shows a prominent right parietal cephalohematoma. There is an ecchymosis at the vitamin K injection site and continuous oozing of blood at the circumcision site. Laboratory studies show:", "labs": [{"rows": [["Hematocrit", "53%"], ["Leukocyte count", "10,500/mm³"], ["  Segmented neutrophils", "50%"], ["  Basophils", "4%"], ["  Lymphocytes", "40%"], ["  Monocytes", "6%"], ["Platelet count", "350,000/mm³"], ["Prothrombin time", "12 sec (INR=1.05)"], ["Partial thromboplastin time", "90 sec"]]}], "stemTail": "Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of these findings?", "options": [["A", "Factor VIII deficiency"], ["B", "Platelet dysfunction"], ["C", "Sepsis"], ["D", "Vitamin K deficiency"], ["E", "von Willebrand factor deficiency"]]},
{"n": 40, "image": null, "stem": "Four hours after an elective cesarean delivery at 38 weeks' gestation, a newborn has nasal flaring and tachypnea. There was no meconium staining of the amniotic fluid. The mother received regular prenatal care, and she had no fever or illness during pregnancy. The newborn's temperature is 37.6°C (99.7°F), pulse is 180/min, and respirations are 80/min. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 88%. The lungs are clear to auscultation. An x-ray of the chest shows prominent pulmonary vascular markings, fluid lines in the fissures, and a flat diaphragm. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [["A", "Aspiration pneumonia"], ["B", "Bacterial pneumonia"], ["C", "Bronchopulmonary dysplasia"], ["D", "Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis"], ["E", "Pulmonary hemorrhage"], ["F", "Respiratory distress syndrome of the newborn"], ["G", "Transient tachypnea of the newborn"]]},
{"n": 41, "image": null, "stem": "A 3714-g (8-lb 3-oz) newborn is delivered at 43 weeks' gestation to a 32-year-old woman, gravida 4, para 3. Apgar scores are 3 and 5 at 1 and 5 minutes, respectively. The newborn's skin and nails are meconium stained, and he is cyanotic. He is immediately intubated and mechanically ventilated at an FiO2 of 100%. No meconium is noted below the vocal cords prior to insertion of the endotracheal tube. One hour later, he remains cyanotic. The lungs are clear to auscultation. Umbilical arterial blood gas analysis shows:", "labs": [{"rows": [["pH", "7.1"], ["PCO2", "34 mm Hg"], ["PO2", "26 mm Hg"]]}], "stemTail": "An x-ray of the chest shows no cardiomegaly or pulmonary infiltrates. Echocardiography shows normal cardiac anatomy with a right-to-left shunt across the foramen ovale. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of these findings?", "options": [["A", "Closure of ductus arteriosus"], ["B", "Closure of foramen ovale"], ["C", "Decreased pulmonary vascular resistance"], ["D", "Failure of pulmonary vasodilation"], ["E", "Increased peripheral vascular resistance"], ["F", "Intracardiac left-to-right shunt"]]},
{"n": 42, "image": "q42", "stem": "A previously healthy 7-year-old girl is brought to the physician by her father because of a 4-month history of episodes of bright red blood in her stool. The episodes are not associated with pain, constipation, or diarrhea. Growth and development are appropriate for age. Her temperature is 37°C (98.6°F), pulse is 90/min, respirations are 18/min, and blood pressure is 110/65 mm Hg. Examination of the face shows areas of hyperpigmentation on the lips and buccal mucosa. A photograph of the mouth is shown. Examination of the anus shows no fissures or signs of trauma. Digital rectal examination shows bright red blood. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in diagnosis?", "options": [["A", "Stool culture"], ["B", "Air-contrast enema"], ["C", "Upper gastrointestinal series"], ["D", "Meckel scan"], ["E", "Colonoscopy"]]},
{"n": 43, "image": null, "stem": "A 16-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department by friends after she passed out at a party. She has a history of substance abuse, and they say that she drank more than eight glasses of alcohol during the past 2 hours. On arrival, she does not respond to painful stimuli. Her temperature is 37.8°C (100°F), pulse is 110/min, respirations are 61/min and shallow, and blood pressure is 110/70 mm Hg. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 90%. Examination shows scleral icterus. The liver edge is palpated 1 cm below the right costal margin. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial step in management?", "options": [["A", "Determine the exact amount and type of alcohol ingested"], ["B", "Measurement of blood alcohol concentration"], ["C", "Nebulized acetylcysteine therapy"], ["D", "Endotracheal intubation"], ["E", "Hemodialysis"]]},
{"n": 44, "image": null, "stem": "A 15-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of a 2-week history of a facial rash and fatigue. She has a 9-day history of stiffness and swelling of her fingers on awakening in the morning. She has no history of serious illness and takes no medications. She appears tired and has mild chest pain with deep inspirations. Her temperature is 38°C (100.4°F), pulse is 94/min and regular, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 112/76 mm Hg. Examination shows a bright red rash over the cheeks and nose. Diffuse 1- to 2-cm lymph nodes are palpated in the anterior and posterior cervical chains. There are three nontender ulcers on the soft palate. The lungs are clear to auscultation. Heart sounds are normal. Examination of the extremities shows mild erythema, tenderness, and swelling of the proximal interphalangeal and metacarpophalangeal joints, knees, and metatarsophalangeal joints. Urinalysis shows 2+ protein and 1+ blood. Which of the following is most appropriate in determining appropriate management for this patient?", "options": [["A", "X-rays of the knees"], ["B", "Echocardiography"], ["C", "MRI of the brain"], ["D", "Cerebral angiography"], ["E", "Renal biopsy"]]},
{"n": 45, "image": null, "stem": "A 3-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of a 2-week history of cough and nasal congestion. Her parents say that she has been tired and irritable for 6 months; during this period, she has been sleeping excessively during the day and snoring loudly. Her pulse is 126/min, respirations are 36/min, and blood pressure is 120/86 mm Hg. On examination, she has nasal congestion and breathes predominantly through her mouth. A grade 1/6 holosystolic murmur is heard in the fourth left parasternal space. There is tonsillar and adenoidal hypertrophy. An x-ray of the chest shows cardiomegaly. Echocardiography shows right ventricular hypertrophy, pulmonary hypertension, and mild tricuspid regurgitation. Which of the following is the most appropriate long-term management?", "options": [["A", "Digoxin and diuretic therapy"], ["B", "Pulmonary vasodilator therapy"], ["C", "Pulmonary artery banding"], ["D", "Adenoidectomy and tonsillectomy"], ["E", "Repair of tricuspid regurgitation"]]},
{"n": 46, "image": null, "stem": "A previously healthy 10-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of a rash over the eyelids and fingers for 1 week. Examination shows a violaceous erythema of the upper eyelids and an erythematous, atrophic, scaly lesion over the knuckles and dorsal surfaces of the finger joints. There is weakness of the proximal extremities and trunk. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [["A", "Dermatomyositis"], ["B", "Eosinophilic granuloma"], ["C", "Inflammatory bowel disease"], ["D", "Juvenile rheumatoid arthritis"], ["E", "Mixed connective tissue disease"], ["F", "Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome (Kawasaki disease)"], ["G", "Periarteritis nodosa"], ["H", "Systemic lupus erythematosus"], ["I", "Systemic sclerosis (scleroderma)"]]},
{"n": 47, "image": "q47", "stem": "An 8-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of a 2-week history of rash and a 1-week history of low-grade fever and a mild headache. The rash began as a small red bump on the right thigh and now measures 12 cm. His temperature is 38.1°C (100.6°F). Examination shows no other abnormalities except for the rash shown. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": [["A", "Atopic dermatitis"], ["B", "Cellulitis"], ["C", "Folliculitis"], ["D", "Herpes simplex"], ["E", "Herpes zoster"], ["F", "Impetigo"], ["G", "Juvenile melanoma"], ["H", "Lyme disease"], ["I", "Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome (Kawasaki disease)"], ["J", "Neurofibromatosis"], ["K", "Nevus flammeus"], ["L", "Poison ivy dermatitis"]]},
{"n": 48, "image": null, "stem": "A previously healthy 6-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department because of severe abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting that began 4 hours after attending a friend's birthday party. Cake and other pastries were served at the party. She appears acutely ill and mildly dehydrated. Her temperature is 37°C (98.6°F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 20/min, and blood pressure is 96/60 mm Hg. Examination shows no abnormalities of the skin. Bowel sounds are hyperactive. Which of the following is the most likely causal organism?", "options": [["A", "Campylobacter jejuni"], ["B", "Clostridium botulinum"], ["C", "Enteroinvasive Escherichia coli"], ["D", "Salmonella enteritidis"], ["E", "Shigella sonnei"], ["F", "Staphylococcus aureus"]]},
{"n": 49, "image": null, "stem": "A 1-month-old girl is brought to the physician because of a 1-day history of decreased oral intake. Her mother says that the child has also had blood in her saliva. She was born at term following an uncomplicated pregnancy and delivery. Examination shows inflammation and erythema of the oral cavity with diffuse white plaques that bleed when scraped. There is a papular erythematous rash with satellite lesions over the diaper area. Which of the following is the most likely immune mechanism for these findings?", "options": [["A", "Decreased B-lymphocyte activity"], ["B", "Decreased dendritic cell activity"], ["C", "Decreased segmented neutrophil function"], ["D", "Decreased T-lymphocyte activity"], ["E", "Increased B-lymphocyte activity"], ["F", "Increased dendritic cell activity"], ["G", "Increased segmented neutrophil function"], ["H", "Increased T-lymphocyte activity"]]},
{"n": 50, "image": null, "stem": "A 14-year-old boy is brought to the physician because his parents are concerned that he has had several episodes of tripping and incoordination over the past 10 months. He has high-arched feet and hammer toes. His father also has high-arched feet and a history of distal extremity weakness. His mother has type 2 diabetes mellitus and severe diabetic neuropathy. Examination of the patient shows mild weakness of the hand musculature and weakness and wasting of the anterior tibial and intrinsic foot muscles. Deep tendon reflexes are absent at the ankles and decreased elsewhere. Sensation to vibration and proprioception are mildly decreased over the distal lower extremities. Motor nerve conduction velocities are markedly decreased with prolonged distal motor latencies in the extremities. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's weakness?", "options": [["A", "Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease"], ["B", "Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy"], ["C", "Diabetic neuropathy"], ["D", "Duchenne muscular dystrophy"], ["E", "Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy"]]}];

