const ANSWER_KEY = {"1": "D", "2": "D", "3": "B", "4": "C", "5": "B", "6": "E", "7": "D", "8": "D", "9": "C", "10": "E", "11": "B", "12": "D", "13": "E", "14": "D", "15": "E", "16": "B", "17": "C", "18": "B", "19": "F", "20": "B", "21": "A", "22": "C", "23": "C", "24": "C", "25": "B", "26": "B", "27": "D", "28": "B", "29": "D", "30": "A", "31": "D", "32": "B", "33": "E", "34": "A", "35": "E", "36": "A", "37": "E", "38": "D", "39": "A", "40": "G", "41": "D", "42": "E", "43": "D", "44": "E", "45": "D", "46": "A", "47": "H", "48": "F", "49": "D", "50": "A"};

const EXPLANATIONS = {
"1": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Gastroenteritis may lead to volume depletion with associated lactic acidosis. Clinical signs of volume depletion include tachycardia, tachypnea, hypotension, dry mucous membranes, poor skin turgor, and prolonged capillary refill. Children may present with irritability or lethargy. Treatment includes the repletion of fluids and electrolytes.</p><p>This infant presents with tachycardia, hypotension, delayed capillary refill, and acute-onset diarrhea consistent with a diagnosis of gastroenteritis with associated volume depletion. Children with gastroenteritis are at increased risk for volume depletion in comparison to adults. Clinical signs of volume depletion include tachycardia, tachypnea, hypotension, dry mucous membranes, poor skin turgor, and prolonged capillary refill. Children may present with irritability or lethargy. Laboratory studies show perturbations in serum sodium and potassium concentrations. Volume depletion secondary to diarrhea is usually accompanied by a significant stool loss of bicarbonate, which, in combination with the hypoperfusion of end organs with lactic acidosis, may result in metabolic acidosis. Urinalysis shows aciduria and an increased urinary specific gravity. Treatment includes the repletion of fluids and electrolytes.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Celiac disease (Choice A), or gluten-sensitive enteropathy, presents with diarrhea and malabsorption caused by gliadin hypersensitivity. It does not present with an acute onset and rarely leads to significant volume depletion.</p><p>Diabetic ketoacidosis (Choice B) may present with volume depletion and metabolic acidosis. However, in an infant this is most commonly caused by type 1 diabetes mellitus, which presents with polyuria and glucosuria. This child's history of acute onset diarrhea and a sick contact are more suggestive of gastroenteritis. Poor feeding in an acutely ill child may lead to starvation ketoacidosis with mild ketonuria.</p><p>Inflammatory bowel disease (Choice C) presents with abdominal pain and bloody diarrhea. It is rare in infancy.</p><p>Renal tubular acidosis (Choice E) is a family of disorders that causes metabolic acidosis. While renal tubular acidosis may present with metabolic acidosis, aciduria, and electrolyte disturbances, it does not typically present with acute onset diarrhea.</p>",
"2": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Decreased caloric intake secondary to prolonged vomiting and diarrhea can lead to hypoglycemia and starvation ketosis. This can cause a metabolic acidosis characterized by decreased bicarbonate and an increased anion gap. Serum and urine ketones will be increased.</p><p>Prolonged vomiting with inability to maintain adequate oral intake and diarrhea can lead to clinically significant levels of dehydration and starvation ketosis. In this case, the child has adequate fluid intake and does not demonstrate signs of dehydration but has had inadequate caloric intake. Starvation ketosis occurs when glycogen stores in the liver have been exhausted, typically after one day of fasting. Following this, blood glucose is maintained primarily through the breakdown of muscle and fat to provide substrates for hepatic gluconeogenesis. After approximately 3 days of fasting, adipose cells and their stored fatty acids become the primary source for energy through the production of ketone bodies. This patient's laboratory values, including profound hypoglycemia, decreased bicarbonate, and increased anion gap are suggestive of starvation ketoacidosis in acute illness, prolonged fasting from persistent vomiting and diarrhea, and inadequate caloric intake. Starvation ketoacidosis is a high anion gap metabolic acidosis. If serum or urine ketones were measured, they would be increased. Production of ketone bodies will continue to provide the brain with an energy source during prolonged fasting.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Adrenal insufficiency (Choice A) may occur in a patient taking chronic glucocorticoids who becomes ill and does not receive any additional glucocorticoid to compensate for the state of acute illness. Insufficient adrenal production of glucocorticoids and mineralocorticoids would cause hypotension, hyperkalemia, and hypernatremia, none of which are present in this patient.</p><p>Fatty acid oxidation defect (Choice B) would prevent formation of ketones. Instead, this patient is successfully producing ketones in response to a starvation state caused by an acute illness. His alert and interactive state supports that he is generating enough fuel for his brain in the form of ketones and thus must have functional fatty acid oxidation.</p><p>Fructose 1-phosphate aldolase deficiency (Choice C) causes hereditary fructose intolerance. This typically presents when fructose-containing foods such as fruit and fruit juice is introduced into the diet. Affected children develop hypoglycemia and vomiting after fructose ingestion. This child's illness is acute and he has not been ingesting fructose.</p><p>Insulin deficiency (Choice E) is the cause of diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). Like starvation ketosis, DKA shows decreased bicarbonate, increased anion gap, and presence of ketones. However, unlike starvation ketosis which demonstrates hypoglycemia, DKA is associated with hyperglycemia.</p>",
"3": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Congenital adrenal hyperplasia results from a defect in adrenal steroid biosynthesis. Its most common form, 21-hydroxylase deficiency, presents with salt wasting in infants or precocious puberty in children. It also causes the virilization of XX infants, increased concentrations of 17-hydroxyprogesterone, hypotension, hyperkalemia, and hypoglycemia. Treatment includes the exogenous administration of mineralocorticoids and glucocorticoids.</p><p>Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) is a defect in adrenal steroid biosynthesis, particularly of cortisol, with variable effects on mineralocorticoids and androgens. Because of decreased production of hormones, the adrenal gland is stimulated by the pituitary in an attempt to increase hormone production, leading to adrenal hyperplasia, the namesake of this condition. The most common form of CAH and the cause of this patient's manifestations is 21-hydroxylase deficiency. 21-hydroxylase deficiency presents with salt wasting in a newborn or precocious puberty in a child, as well as virilization of infants with an XX karyotype (eg, clitoral hypertrophy, labial fusion), caused by increased concentrations of androgens. Because of the enzyme deficiency, concentrations of 17-hydroxyprogesterone, androstenedione, and testosterone are increased, while aldosterone and cortisol are decreased, leading to hypotension, hyperkalemia, and hypoglycemia (caused by inadequate cortisol). Diffuse hyperpigmentation of the skin, as seen in this newborn, is characteristic. Melanin production is regulated in part by melanocyte-stimulating hormone (MSH). MSH is a byproduct of proopiomelanocortin (POMC) which also produces adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH). In adrenal insufficiency, stimulation of ACTH production by POMC simultaneously produces MSH, leading to diffuse hyperpigmentation in affected patients. Treatment of CAH consists of exogenous glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid supplementation.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, E, and F.</p><p>When present, aldosterone binds to receptors in the collecting tubule of the nephron and leads to placement of sodium and potassium channels on the luminal cell membrane. Potassium exits the cell through the channel and into the lumen. Without aldosterone, potassium does not leave the cell and instead accumulates in the interstitium and blood, causing increased serum potassium, not decreased serum potassium (Choices A, C, and E).</p><p>Without elimination of potassium, there is no gradient by which sodium reenters the cell. Instead, sodium remains in the lumen and is lost in the urine. This is called salt wasting and is accompanied by a decreased serum sodium, not normal (Choice F) or increased sodium (Choice D).</p>",
"4": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Children with developmental delay should undergo audiometry to evaluate for hearing loss. Failure to detect hearing loss can cause preventable delays in a child's language, social, and educational development. The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends routine audiometry screening for all school-aged children.</p><p>Children with developmental delay should undergo audiometry to evaluate for hearing loss. Failure to detect hearing loss can cause preventable delays in a child's language, social, and educational development. Hearing loss in children may occur because of either conductive or sensorineural etiologies. A careful history and general physical examination should be performed to evaluate for possible syndromic features. Weber and Rinne tests can distinguish conductive and sensorineural hearing loss. The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends routine audiometry screening for all school-aged children.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Intelligence testing (Choice A) is not an appropriate initial test for isolated delay in language development. This child's isolated delay in language development with otherwise normal developmental milestones is more concerning for hearing loss.</p><p>Speech evaluation (Choice B) may be useful for identifying abnormalities of speech production. This child has a low-normal vocabulary for his age. While speech evaluation may be warranted, audiometry should be performed first.</p><p>MRI of the inner ears (Choice D) may be indicated based on the results of audiometry and a careful physical examination but is not an appropriate initial test.</p><p>No further evaluation is indicated at this time (Choice E) is incorrect. Failure to detect hearing loss can cause preventable delays in a child's language, social, and educational development.</p>",
"5": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: N. gonorrhoeae can present with vaginitis, cervicitis, and pelvic inflammatory disease. Following a high-risk exposure, empiric treatment with ceftriaxone and doxycycline, with the latter covering a possible concurrent C. trachomatis infection, is appropriate while confirmatory tests are pending.</p><p>Neisseria gonorrhoeae is a Gram-negative diplococcus and can cause vaginitis, cervicitis, and pelvic inflammatory disease (PID). Vaginitis typically presents with mucopurulent discharge and pruritus. Cervicitis presents with similar discharge, but also with an erythematous, friable cervix and cervical motion tenderness on examination. When infection spreads to the fallopian tubes and ovaries, PID with purulent cervical discharge, uterine and adnexal tenderness, and systemic symptoms such as fever can result. If left untreated, PID can become complicated by a tuba-ovarian abscess or intraabdominal abscess, presenting with focal tenderness and a fluctuant mass; it can cause consequent infertility. Gonorrhea can present with dissemination (tenosynovitis, dermatitis, polyarthralgia) or reactive arthritis. Risk factors for the development of gonorrhea include multiple sexual partners, new sexual partners, receptive anal intercourse, a young age, substance abuse, and inconsistent condom usage. Coinfection with Chlamydia trachomatis is common, therefore treatment typically consists of ceftriaxone and doxycycline, with the latter covering a possible concurrent chlamydial infection while confirmatory tests are pending. In a high-risk exposure to gonorrhea, empiric treatment to cover both N. gonorrhoeae and C. trachomatis is appropriate.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</p><p>Intramuscular ceftriaxone therapy only (Choice A) would treat gonorrhea. However, risk for coinfection with C. trachomatis is high, and this antibiotic regimen would not cover a chlamydial infection.</p><p>Oral azithromycin therapy only (Choice C) would treat a chlamydial infection. However, this patient has been exposed to gonorrhea and requires additional treatment with ceftriaxone.</p><p>Oral azithromycin and intramuscular penicillin therapy (Choice D) would cover a chlamydial infection but would not cover gonorrhea, as N. gonorrhoeae has become increasingly resistant to penicillin. Intramuscular ceftriaxone and oral doxycycline would provide better coverage.</p><p>No treatment indicated because the patient is asymptomatic (Choice E) is inappropriate, as failure to diagnose and treat this patient could lead to a multitude of complications, such as reactive arthritis, disseminated gonococcal infection, and pelvic inflammatory disease with consequent infertility. She has had a high-risk exposure to gonorrhea and empiric treatment is more appropriate.</p>",
"6": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The appendix testis is a vestigial structure, typically on the anterior and superior aspect of the testis and has a pedunculated shape that may predispose it to torsion. Patients with torsion of the appendix testis present with groin pain, a nontender testicle, a tender palpable localized mass adjacent to the testicle, and an intact cremasteric reflex.</p><p>Testicular torsion occurs when the testicle rotates about itself within the scrotal sac, resulting in vascular congestion and potential cessation of venous or arterial flow to the testis. Patients typically present with acute, severe testicular pain with diffuse tenderness and swelling and an absent cremasteric reflex. In contrast, patients with torsion of the appendix testis present with groin pain, a nontender testicle, a tender, palpable localized mass adjacent to one pole of the testicle, and an intact cremasteric reflex. The appendix testis is a vestigial structure, typically on the anterior and superior aspect of the testis and has a pedunculated shape that may predispose it to torsion. When torsed, the appendix testis may appear as a small blue or black mass in the scrotum. The diagnosis may be made clinically, or with ultrasound in cases where testicular torsion cannot be excluded. While patients with testicular torsion require surgical detorsion and orchiopexy to prevent infertility, management of torsion of the appendix testis is generally supportive, with analgesics and scrotal support.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Epididymitis (Choice A) is a common cause of painful scrotal swelling and refers to acute infection and inflammation of the epididymis. In younger males, this is commonly secondary to sexually transmitted infections such as Chlamydia trachomatis or Neisseria gonorrhoeae. Patients with epididymitis may have moderate pain relief with elevation of the testis.</p><p>Incarcerated hernia (Choice B) refers to the contents of a hernia becoming trapped in the hernia defect, unable to be reduced to native position. If this type of hernia contains bowel, an obstruction can result. An inguinal hernia can become incarcerated, but this would typically present with severe tenderness and a larger mass with swelling in the inguinal area.</p><p>Orchitis (Choice C) is inflammation of the testes that results from an infection, sometimes from the paramyxovirus that causes mumps. Clinical manifestations include fever, myalgias, and fatigue. Especially when it occurs after puberty, it is a risk factor for infertility.</p><p>Testicular hematoma (Choice D) can occur in blunt scrotal trauma and presents with testicular swelling, warmth, and pain. A 3-mm area of bluish discoloration is more likely caused by torsion of an appendix testis, not testicular hematoma, especially with no history of trauma.</p>",
"7": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Patients with X-linked agammaglobulinemia have mutations in the BTK gene, which encodes a tyrosine kinase essential for all stages of B lymphocyte development and proliferation. They present with recurrent infections and decreased or absent concentrations of all immunoglobulin subclasses. Treatment is with intravenous immune globulin.</p><p>Monthly infusions of immune globulin should be given to this patient with a likely diagnosis of X-linked agammaglobulinemia. X-linked agammaglobulinemia is defined by defects in the Bruton tyrosine kinase (BTK) gene, which is necessary for all stages of B lymphocyte development and proliferation. Deficiency of this enzyme results in failed B lymphocyte development. As B lymphocytes are responsible for secreting immunoglobulins once they differentiate into plasma cells, a defect in B lymphocyte maturation leads to a dearth of plasma cells and severely decreased immunoglobulin concentrations of every class, including IgM, IgG, IgE, and IgA. Patients present with recurrent infections caused by impaired humoral immunity, and treatment is with intravenous immunoglobulin concentrates.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Amoxicillin prophylaxis (Choice A) is given to patients with congenital heart disease or those with a history of endocarditis who are undergoing certain procedures. While amoxicillin would likely treat his recurrent episodes of otitis media, it would not correct the underlying problem.</p><p>Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor therapy (Choice B) is used to stimulate production of granulocytes in patients receiving cytotoxic chemotherapy; it is not the treatment for agammaglobulinemia.</p><p>Oral prednisone therapy (Choice C) results in immunosuppression and an increased risk for infections. This would not be an appropriate therapeutic option for this patient.</p><p>Splenectomy (Choice E) is employed occasionally for patients with refractory hereditary spherocytosis who develop debilitating anemia. Splenectomy generally increases the risk for infection with encapsulated organisms such as Haemophilus influenzae, Neisseria meningitidis, and Streptococcus pneumoniae.</p>",
"8": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Opioid intoxication causes symptoms of CNS depression including sedation, respiratory depression, bradycardia, and hypotension. Miosis differentiates opioid intoxication from many other toxidromes.</p><p>This patient is intoxicated with oxycodone, an opioid medication. Opioids are central nervous system (CNS) depressants used as analgesics and recreational drugs. Opioids act throughout the CNS and peripheral nervous system and lead to euphoria (caused by interaction with dopamine), altered mental status, sedation, bradycardia (70/min is bradycardia in a 2-year-old patient), hypotension, depressed respiratory drive, and constricted pupils. Specifically, the µ2 opioid receptor, located in the brainstem respiratory centers and on peripheral chemo- and baroreceptors, controls respiratory depression, which may be severe. Miosis is a distinctive finding that is less common in intoxication with other CNS depressants; it is caused by direct opioid receptor activity in the brain areas responsible for pupillary control. Opioids also act on receptors within the enteric nervous system, reducing gut motility and causing constipation. The treatment of opioid toxicity includes supportive care (eg, respiratory support) and naloxone, a short-acting opioid receptor antagonist.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Aspirin overdose (Choice A) presents with respiratory alkalosis from salicylate stimulus of the medullary respiratory centers, along with high anion gap metabolic acidosis. Tinnitus may also be present. Though depressed mental status may be seen, respiratory depression, bradycardia, and pupillary constriction are more consistent with opioid intoxication.</p><p>Imipramine overdose (Choice B) typically leads to cardiovascular toxicity (eg, QTc and QRS prolongation), CNS toxicity (eg, sedation, coma, seizures), and anticholinergic toxicity (eg, pupillary dilation, dry mouth, tachycardia, urinary retention). Pupillary constriction and bradycardia would be atypical.</p><p>Iron toxicity (Choice C) may lead to free radical production and consequent gastrointestinal distress (eg, pain, nausea, diarrhea, and ultimately bowel obstruction), shock and metabolic acidosis, and hepatotoxicity. Pupillary constriction and bradycardia would be atypical.</p><p>Phenobarbital (Choice E) is a barbiturate, a CNS depressant that has similar effects in toxicity to alcohol and benzodiazepines. The risk for dangerous respiratory depression is higher in barbiturates than benzodiazepines or alcohol. Pupils are typically a normal size.</p>",
"9": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Rheumatic fever is a sequela of untreated group A Streptococcus infection, commonly pharyngitis, in which there is a type II hypersensitivity reaction affecting the heart. Severe cardiac damage, including mitral valve regurgitation, prolapse, or stenosis may occur, especially if the rheumatic fever is untreated or recurrent. Penicillin is useful for preventing the development and recurrence of rheumatic fever.</p><p>This girl presents with rheumatic fever, characterized by arthritis and mitral regurgitation, following an episode streptococcal pharyngitis. Rheumatic fever is a sequela of untreated group A Streptococcus infection, commonly pharyngitis, in which there is a type II hypersensitivity reaction to self-antigens having a similar structure to, and thus molecularly mimicking streptococcal M protein. Diagnosis is made by the Jones criteria, which describe many of the core clinical features of rheumatic fever. The major criteria include polyarthritis, carditis, subcutaneous nodules, erythema marginatum, and Sydenham chorea; the minor criteria include fever, leukocytosis, arthralgia, and PR interval prolongation. Patients often have an increased antistreptolysin O titer. Biopsy of affected cardiac tissue discloses a granulomatous reaction with presence of Aschoff bodies. Complications of rheumatic fever include mitral valve inflammation leading to long-term sequelae of chronic mitral valve regurgitation, prolapse, or stenosis. Treatment of the initial streptococcal infection is effective for reducing the risk for developing rheumatic fever. Per American Heart Association (AHA) guidelines, once rheumatic fever has developed, treatment includes salicylates and a prolonged course of monthly penicillin treatments to reduce the risk for recurrence and further valvular damage.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Clindamycin (Choice A) and erythromycin (Choice B) are appropriate agents for the primary prevention of rheumatic fever in patients with penicillin allergies but are not most appropriate for secondary prevention of rheumatic fever recurrence in patients without penicillin allergies.</p><p>Prednisone (Choice D), a corticosteroid, may be useful for reducing inflammation in rheumatic fever, but it does not help prevent disease recurrence. Salicylates reduce inflammation and pain associated with rheumatic fever.</p><p>No pharmacotherapy is indicated (Choice E) is incorrect. Failure to treat with penicillin increases the patient's risk for recurrences of rheumatic fever with associated mitral valve damage.</p>",
"10": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: In a mother without prenatal care and an unknown HBsAg status, the newborn should be given the first dose of HBV. HBIG should be given if the maternal HBsAg assay returns positive or remains unknown, but not if it returns negative. In either case, the newborn should receive the full HBV series, which includes additional doses at ages 2 and 6 months.</p><p>The recommended schedule of hepatitis B immunization and immunoprophylaxis in newborns is dependent on the maternal HBsAg status. HBsAg assay is an important component of prenatal care and is typically checked at the first prenatal visit. If HBsAg is positive or if there are signs of active maternal hepatitis B infection, the newborn should receive the first dose of hepatitis B vaccine (HBV) intramuscularly and hepatitis B immune globulin (HBIG) as early as possible, with additional doses of HBV at 2 and 6 months of age. In a HBsAg negative mother, only HBV is needed and doses are given at birth, 2 months, and 6 months. As in this case, if the mother's HBsAg status is unknown but there is no evidence of active infection, the newborn should receive HBV within 12 hours and HBIG can be delayed until the maternal serologies have returned. If the serology returns positive for HBsAg, the infant should receive HBIG within 1 week. If the serology returns negative for HBsAg, the newborn does not require HBIG. In either case, the newborn will still require the second and third doses of HBV at 2 and 6 months, respectively.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D. Observation only (Choice A) without follow-up of the maternal HBsAg titer is not appropriate. If the titer returns positive or unknown, HBIG should be given.</p><p>HBIG only now (Choice B) is not necessary. Administration of HBIG to a neonate born to a hepatitis B infected mother may occur within the first week of life, which provides adequate time for the maternal HBsAg assay to return.</p><p>Even if the maternal HBsAg assay is positive, HBIG at 2 and 6 months of age (Choice C) is not necessary. HBV should be given at these time points but HBIG is only needed at birth.</p><p>HBV at 2 and 6 months of age (Choice D) will be inadequate if the maternal HBsAg titer is positive.</p>",
"11": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Central diabetes insipidus may result from head trauma and is characterized by the inadequate secretion of ADH from the posterior pituitary. Patients typically present with large volumes of dilute urine, dehydration, thirst, and increased serum osmolality.</p><p>Central diabetes insipidus may result from head trauma and is characterized by the inadequate secretion of antidiuretic hormone (ADH, also known as vasopressin) from the posterior pituitary. ADH binds V2 receptors of the late distal convoluted tubule and the collecting tubule principal cells, which results in the insertion of aquaporin channels in the luminal surface. This results in increased free water absorption and maintains tight regulation of serum osmolality. Inadequate production of ADH by the posterior pituitary gland may occur in various pathologies, such as pituitary tumors, trauma, tuberculosis, or intracranial surgery. This results in a decreased concentration of aquaporin channels inserted within the principal cells of the collecting tubule, leading to an inability to absorb adequate amounts of free water, which causes dilute urine, decreased urine osmolality, hypernatremia, and increased serum osmolality. The hypothalamic osmoreceptors detect this increased serum osmolality, which causes the patient to report extreme thirst. Patients typically also present with production of large volumes of dilute urine, dehydration, and altered mental status. Treatment is with desmopressin, an analog of vasopressin, which results in recovery of free water via aquaporin insertion in the collecting tubule.</p><p>Incorrect Answers:A, C, D, E, and F.</p><p>Chronic renal failure (Choice A) can have variable etiology, and in children it may be caused by intrinsic renal disease such as polycystic kidney disease, chronic nephritic or nephrotic glomerular disease, or congenital urinary tract abnormalities leading to postrenal obstructive disease. It would be unlikely in this patient with normal creatinine and no history of kidney impairment.</p><p>Diabetes mellitus (Choice C) can occur in children because of autoimmune destruction of pancreatic islet cells, leading to impaired insulin production and hyperglycemia. In this patient with normal glucose concentrations and imbalanced serum and urine osmolality, central diabetes insipidus is more likely.</p><p>Syndrome of inappropriate secretion of ADH (vasopressin) (Choice D) can occur secondary to head trauma, but involves excess ADH production, which leads to euvolemic hyponatremia.</p><p>Psychogenic polydipsia (Choice E) refers to excess intake of free water. Excess free water consumption dilutes urea and sodium which are lost in diuresis; the resultant lack of concentration of sodium and urea within the renal interstitium results in loss of ability to concentrate urine. This results in hyponatremia, decreased serum osmolality, and decreased urine osmolality.</p><p>Urinary tract infection (Choice F) can present with dysuria, urinary frequency, foul-smelling urine, suprapubic discomfort, and fever. In pediatric patients, it can also cause nocturia, but this patient does not have any other signs of a urinary tract infection. It would not cause hypernatremia and increased serum osmolality.</p>",
"12": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) refers to an abnormally rapid pulse and rhythm originating from above the ventricles, typically with a narrow QRS complex on ECG.</p><p>Supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) refers to an abnormally rapid heart rate and rhythm originating from above the ventricles, typically with a regular rhythm and a narrow QRS complex on ECG. Patients with SVT can manifest with acute symptoms of lightheadedness, palpitations, diaphoresis, and syncope. In infants, signs and symptoms can include pallor, irritability, poor feeding, and cyanosis. Infants who present with SVT typically have a pulse between 220 and 280/min. SVT may be caused by structural defects in the His-Purkinje system (eg, Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome), or it may be provoked by alcohol, nicotine, caffeine, or psychological stress. In this patient, it may have been provoked by infection or use of an over-the-counter cough and cold preparation, which can contain pseudoephedrine, a sympathomimetic drug that can be used as a nasal decongestant. Because of its sympathomimetic effects, it can cause adverse reactions of tachycardia, anxiety, and palpitations. In patients who are hemodynamically unstable, cardioversion is indicated in the management of SVT. For patients who are hemodynamically stable, attempts can be made to terminate the tachyarrhythmia by vagal maneuvers (eg, ice or cold water over the face) or with adenosine.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Atrial fibrillation (Choice A) can occur with a rapid ventricular rate but typically presents with irregular tachycardia. Atrial fibrillation also occurs more often in patients with structural heart disease, and supraventricular tachycardia is more likely in a previously healthy patient with regular tachycardia.</p><p>Long QT interval syndrome (Choice B) is a disorder of prolonged ventricular depolarization and repolarization. It appears on ECG with a prolonged QT interval. It can be complicated by polymorphic ventricular tachycardia or torsades de pointes.</p><p>Sinus tachycardia (Choice C) would be unlikely to have a pulse of 260/min. The maximal pulse from the sinus node is typically lower than 220/min and on ECG, there are P waves prior to every QRS complex. Pulse resulting from supraventricular tachycardia can be higher because of a reentrant rhythm that bypasses the atrioventricular node.</p><p>Ventricular tachycardia (VT) (Choice E) shows a wide QRS complex tachycardia on ECG. VT occurs when an abnormal electrical focus or a reentry circuit causes rapid contraction of the ventricles. There are many criteria for defining VT on the rhythm strip, but the general morphology will show wide QRS complex tachycardia. There are monomorphic and polymorphic forms of VT. Treatment depends on the duration, type, and symptomatology.</p>",
"13": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Leukocoria in an infant raises concern for retinoblastoma. Retinoblastoma is a malignant proliferation of retinal neuroendocrine cells and is the most common ophthalmic malignancy in children. It results from biallelic, inherited or acquired, inactivating mutations of the RB1 gene, a tumor suppressor. Treatment is with chemotherapy or enucleation.</p><p>This child presents with leukocoria (a white pupillary reflex) and a sensory exotropia, which is suggestive of retinoblastoma. Retinoblastoma is a malignant proliferation of retinal neuroendocrine cells and is the most common ophthalmic malignancy in children. Retinoblastoma results from inactivating mutations of both copies of the RB1 gene, which functions as a tumor suppressor by halting cell cycle progression at the G1 checkpoint. RB1 mutations may be either acquired or inherited. In cases of germline RB1 mutation, a single mutated allele is inherited, and the second allele acquires a subsequent mutation during development. Infants who carry germline mutations are more likely to have bilateral retinoblastoma with multiple foci of tumors in the eye, whereas infants with two acquired mutations are more likely to have unilateral retinoblastoma. Ultrasound of the eye is helpful for defining the size of the lesion and for detecting characteristic intralesional calcifications. Within the eye, large tumor size, tumor seeding of the vitreous humor, and involvement of the iris or anterior chamber are associated with a worse prognosis. If undetected, retinoblastoma may invade the optic nerve, leading to a greater risk for metastasis to the brain. Most cases of retinoblastoma may be successfully managed with chemotherapy, although advanced cases may require enucleation with special attention paid to achieving negative margins at the optic nerve, as cases that have undergone metastasis have a poor prognosis.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Acquired exotropia (Choice A) may result from visual sensory deprivation in a non-seeing eye. However, exotropia will not cause leukocoria.</p><p>Chronic endophthalmitis (Choice B) typically results from hematogenous spread of bacteria or fungus or following ocular surgery. This infant is not ill-appearing and lacks a history suggestive of a likely cause of endophthalmitis. Eyes affected by endophthalmitis are often painful and have conjunctival injection.</p><p>Congenital glaucoma (Choice C) presents with characteristic findings including buphthalmos (an enlarged eye), a large cornea, which may be edematous and opacified, and increased intraocular pressure. Infants typically present with epiphora, photophobia, and blepharospasm or eye rubbing. Congenital glaucoma does not cause leukocoria.</p><p>Familial cataract (Choice D) may be either unilateral or bilateral and may be associated with chromosomal abnormalities or inherited mutations in a variety of genes. Congenital cataracts are present at birth. This child has developed a wandering eye after normal development for the first several months of life, which is more consistent with a diagnosis of retinoblastoma.</p>",
"14": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Guttate psoriasis is a form of psoriasis in which small, scaly papules and plaques develop diffusely over the trunk and extremities. It is often triggered by a streptococcal infection. Nail pitting and onycholysis may be seen in all types of psoriasis, including guttate.</p><p>Guttate psoriasis is characterized by numerous papules and small plaques scattered all over the trunk and extremities. Each individual papule or plaque has a small amount of white scale over the top. Like psoriasis vulgaris, removal of the white scale causes pinpoint bleeding (Auspitz sign). This is because of the thinning of the epidermis over the dermal capillaries, which are also dilated in psoriasis. Removal of the scales also causes Koebner phenomenon, the development of new lesions at sites of trauma. All forms of psoriasis, including guttate psoriasis, may be accompanied by nail changes. The most common nail change in psoriasis is pitting, which is seen in this patient, but onycholysis (splitting of the nail plate from the nail bed) is also common. Presence or absence of nail involvement is a useful physical examination consideration when evaluating any full body rash. Guttate psoriasis is often triggered by a streptococcal infection or by medications, though neither of these triggers are relevant in this case. Treatment of guttate psoriasis includes topical therapies such as corticosteroids and vitamin D analogs. If the psoriasis is refractory to topical therapies, then phototherapy, methotrexate, or biologic medications may be considered.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Cellulitis (Choice A) presents with cutaneous pain, erythema, and induration spreading outward from a source of infection, often an initial abrasion or injection. The numerous papules and small plaques seen in this case are characteristic of guttate psoriasis, not cellulitis. However, a streptococcal cellulitis could potentially trigger guttate psoriasis.</p><p>Nummular eczema (Choice B) is a term used to describe eczematous patches which are round or coin shaped. Like other forms of eczema, or dermatitis, the rash is initially characterized by erythematous papules and vesicles with exudates and crusting. With chronic scratching, the affected areas become dry, scaly, and excoriated in a process called lichenification.</p><p>Pityriasis rosea (Choice C) is a cutaneous eruption characterized by the acute onset of a pruritic patch, which is followed by the development of numerous smaller macules and patches. It commonly involves the trunk and proximal extremities. It is usually induced by a viral infection including human herpesvirus 6 and 7, though often the exact infection is not determined. In this case, a pruritic patch was not seen making guttate psoriasis more likely than pityriasis rosea.</p><p>Tinea corporis (Choice E) is caused by a superficial dermatophyte infection of the skin, most often Trichophyton tonsurans. The classic appearance is a round, annular patch with a scaly border. Potassium hydroxide preparation will show hyphae. Numerous small papules and plaques are not typically seen.</p>",
"15": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Children with foreign body aspiration can present with severe respiratory distress, cyanosis, and stridor, or they may have a less severe presentation with cough, tachypnea, decreased air entry on auscultation, or wheezing caused by a partial airway obstruction.</p><p>Wheezing that does not improve with nebulized albuterol, hyperinflation of the right lung, and acute onset of difficulty breathing and cough raises suspicion for foreign body aspiration. Foreign body aspiration can cause obstruction of the airway, leading to impaired oxygenation and ventilation. Children from 1 to 3 years old are at increased risk for foreign body aspiration because of their ability to stand and mobilize independently, ability to grasp and put small objects into their mouths, and an immature swallowing mechanism. Foreign bodies can be located anywhere in the airway between the larynx and the bronchi. Children can present with severe respiratory distress, cyanosis, and stridor, or may have a less severe presentation with partial airway obstruction, presenting with cough, tachypnea, decreased air entry on auscultation, or wheezing. Chest x-ray can show hyperinflation of one lung, which is caused by air trapping and partial airway obstruction in which air is able to pass via inspiration, but not exhalation. Foreign bodies are typically in the right lung because of the more vertical anatomic position of the right main bronchus. Management of foreign body aspiration involves flexible or rigid bronchoscopy to remove the object. Object removal is necessary and retained foreign bodies can be complicated by airway inflammation and infection.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Chest percussion and physiotherapy (Choice A) can be beneficial for patients with mucus plugging of small airways, such as in cystic fibrosis. Chest percussion can help dislodge mucus and improve its clearance by coughing.</p><p>Oral administration of amoxicillin (Choice B) would be indicated if the patient presented with a bacterial illness, such as pneumonia, streptococcal pharyngitis, or acute otitis media. Patients with pneumonia can also present with cough, however it is typically a subacute onset and a chest x-ray would show localized infiltrate or opacification in an area of the lung.</p><p>Oral administration of corticosteroids (Choice C) would be helpful in a patient with moderate to severe acute asthma exacerbation along with nebulized albuterol. Corticosteroids reduce inflammation of airways and have been shown to decrease hospitalizations for asthma. This patient's wheezing did not improve with nebulized albuterol, making acute asthma exacerbation less likely.</p><p>Subcutaneous administration of epinephrine (Choice D) would not be indicated in this patient. Intramuscular epinephrine is used in the emergent treatment of anaphylaxis, which can be characterized by urticaria, vomiting, diarrhea, wheezing, stridor, oropharyngeal swelling, and airway obstruction. However, this patient's wheezing is more likely caused by foreign body aspiration and the patient has no other signs of anaphylaxis. Furthermore, intramuscular epinephrine is preferred over subcutaneous administration because of its more rapid and reliable onset.</p>",
"16": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease refers to idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head, which typically presents in young boys with a limp and hip pain. It requires early recognition and intervention to prevent long-term deformity.</p><p>Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease refers to idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head that most commonly occurs in younger boys aged 4 to 9 years. The classic presentation includes slow onset of hip pain which is worsened by activity and associated with a limp. The pain may or may not be relieved with rest. X-rays often show no abnormalities. However, bone scan characteristically shows decreased perfusion of the femoral head, as in this case. MRI may also be considered for diagnosis and will show bone marrow changes. Treatments include preventing weight-bearing on the affected leg and, occasionally, may require operative intervention or hip arthroplasty. Inadequate treatment results in fragmentation and subsequent deformity of the femoral head.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, E, F, and G.</p><p>Ankylosing spondylitis (Choice A) is a rheumatologic inflammatory condition involving the vertebrae that presents with subacute to chronic back pain and limited range of motion that is improved with exercise. The resultant arthropathy can result in frequent falls and fractures, however without back pain or any systemic inflammatory manifestations, it would be an unlikely diagnosis in this case.</p><p>Pauciarticular juvenile rheumatoid arthritis (Choice C), also called oligoarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis, is most common in girls aged 2 to 3 years. It presents with swelling of a single joint, often the knee, with accompanied limping but no pain. On examination, the involved joints are swollen and tender without erythema. Unlike other forms of juvenile idiopathic arthritis, systemic manifestations are characteristically absent.</p><p>Septic arthritis (Choice D) results from bacterial infection of the synovial joint space, most commonly by Staphylococcus aureus. The affected joint demonstrates pain, swelling, and erythema. Arthrocentesis is indicated for the evaluation of monoarticular erythema and swelling to evaluate for septic arthritis, which characteristically shows purulent synovial fluid with greater than 50,000 leukocytes per mm³.</p><p>Slipped capital femoral epiphysis (Choice E) most commonly occurs in overweight children between the ages of 10 to 15 years. It results in displacement of the femoral epiphysis relative to the femoral neck secondary to anterosuperior movement of the metaphysis caused by a fracture of the growth plate.</p><p>Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) (Choice F) is an autoimmune disease resulting in a constellation of symptoms including fever, rash, photosensitivity, renal disease, and nonerosive arthritis. It is more common in female patients and isolated monoarticular joint involvement is inadequate for diagnosis.</p><p>Toxic synovitis (Choice G), also known as transient synovitis, is a non-infectious arthropathy that presents with pain in a joint, often the hip, frequently following a viral infection in a child. It is unlikely in this child who has not had any recent illness and has decreased uptake on bone scan.</p>",
"17": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Sepsis can be complicated by shock, hypotension, and multiorgan failure with an increased mortality rate. The initial step for management in septic shock involves emergent antibiotics and fluid resuscitation with isotonic crystalloids, such as 0.9% saline.</p><p>Meningitis in infants is commonly caused by bacteria and can present with fever or hypothermia, lethargy, irritability, altered mental status, a bulging fontanelle, poor feeding, and seizures. Diagnosis of meningitis typically involves lumbar puncture, and evaluation of the cerebrospinal fluid will show an increased white blood cell count with predominant neutrophils, increased protein, and decreased glucose. Thorough evaluation also includes a complete blood count, blood culture, and urine culture to evaluate for other causes of sepsis, as well as culture of the cerebrospinal fluid to determine the causative organism. This patient has signs of sepsis, a systemic inflammatory syndrome that may occur in response to infection. Sepsis can be complicated by shock and multiorgan failure with a high mortality rate. Septic shock is characterized by vasoplegia (impaired response of the vasculature to vasoconstricting stimuli) with markedly decreased systemic vascular resistance, tachycardia, increased cardiac output, oliguria, and lactic acidosis. The initial step for management in septic shock involves emergency antibiotics and fluid resuscitation with isotonic crystalloids, such as 0.9% saline.</p><p>Incorrect Answers:A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Administration of dopamine (Choice A) or epinephrine (Choice B) may be indicated in this patient if the patient continues to be hypotensive after a trial of fluid resuscitation. Fluid administration should be the initial step for resuscitation of septic shock.</p><p>Administration of sodium bicarbonate (Choice D) would be unlikely to be beneficial in this patient, as this patient's pH is normal. Administration of sodium bicarbonate may be helpful in patients who are acidotic.</p><p>Endotracheal intubation (Choice E) would not be emergently indicated in this patient as the patient is breathing, ventilating, and oxygenating well as shown on arterial blood gas analysis. The patient's hypotension and hemodynamic instability would also cause increased risk for cardiac arrest if endotracheal intubation were attempted.</p>",
"18": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Aplastic anemia can be acquired following a viral infection and presents with normocytic, normochromic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and leukopenia. Bone marrow biopsy and aspirate is required for diagnosis and will show a paucity of progenitor cells with marrow replacement by fat in the absence of fibrosis or malignant infiltration.</p><p>Aplastic anemia (AA) is the most likely cause of this patient's normocytic anemia, leukopenia, and thrombocytopenia with normal serum iron following infection with hepatitis A. AA can either be inherited or acquired. Acquired AA may follow exposure to certain types of viruses (eg, hepatitis, Epstein-Barr virus, or HIV), chemicals (eg, benzene), or medications (eg, methimazole, sulfa antibiotics, or chloramphenicol), or it may be idiopathic. The method of bone marrow progenitor destruction is thought to be autoimmune. Patients who develop AA after a viral infection usually present with pancytopenia within 6 months after diagnosis, but symptoms related to pancytopenia such as fatigue, exercise intolerance, pallor, and petechiae, often appear sooner. Diagnosis includes bone marrow biopsy and aspiration, which will show a profoundly hypocellular bone marrow with a paucity of progenitor cells of all lineages with replacement by fat and stroma. Importantly, malignant cells and fibrosis will be absent. Treatment is either with immunosuppressive medications or hematopoietic stem cell transplant from a matched sibling if available.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, E, F, G, H, and I.</p><p>Anemia, acute hemorrhagic, (Choice A) describes massive blood loss that leads to anemia. This is common in trauma and presents with anemia in the absence of leukopenia or thrombocytopenia.</p><p>Anemia, autoimmune hemolytic (Choice C), results from the binding of an autoantibody to red blood cell surface antigens, leading to removal via the reticuloendothelial system or to intravascular hemolysis. Diagnosis is made by a positive Coombs test, unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, decreased haptoglobin, and the presence of spherocytes and reticulocytes on the peripheral smear.</p><p>Anemia, chronic disease (Choice D), is a common finding in autoimmune and inflammatory disease, chronic infections, and malignancy. Increased hepcidin concentrations decrease iron absorption and increase iron storage in the bone marrow, preventing use of iron by erythrocyte precursors and resulting in anemia.</p><p>Glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency (Choice E) results in hemolysis upon exposure to certain medications such as sulfa drugs and oxidants. It does not cause marked leukopenia or thrombocytopenia.</p><p>Hereditary spherocytosis (Choice F) is caused by autosomal dominant mutations in ankyrin, spectrin, and band erythrocyte membrane proteins that lead to a loss of the normal connection between the inner membrane skeleton and the outer lipid bilayer. Clinical symptoms can include jaundice, splenomegaly, and anemia. Peripheral smear shows spherocytes.</p><p>Sickle cell disease (Choice G) is caused by mutations in both β-globin alleles that result in an abnormal hemoglobin S, which polymerizes in hypoxia and causes sickling of erythrocytes. Patients have anemia and sickle forms on peripheral blood smear.</p><p>Sickle cell trait (Choice H) is caused by a mutation in one β-globin allele. It infrequently causes symptoms associated with sickle cell disease such as severe anemia and acute pain crisis. Anemia, if present, is mild. Other cell lines show no abnormalities.</p><p>Thalassemia minor (Choice I) can occur as a result of mutations or deletions in one of the β-globin alleles or in one or more of the α-globin alleles, leading to impaired production of hemoglobin A. Patients have a mild microcytic anemia and normal concentrations of leukocytes and platelets. Hemoglobin electrophoresis can distinguish between these two entities.</p>",
"19": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Induration exceeding 15 mm is considered a positive result for a PPD skin test. Risk factors for tuberculosis include exposure to crowded conditions, as in a skilled nursing care facility or prison, residence or travel in an endemic area, and causes of immunocompromise, such as diabetes mellitus, alcoholism, or HIV infection.</p><p>Induration exceeding 15 mm is considered a positive result for a PPD skin test and is suggestive of latent tuberculosis infection in this patient. Mycobacterium tuberculosis is a slow-growing mycobacterial organism transmitted via respiratory secretions. It presents with primary, latent, and reactivation patterns. In primary disease, patients present with subacute fevers, weight loss, night sweats, cough, and malaise. Latent tuberculosis can occur as the organism proliferates within macrophages and is maintained within caseating granulomas. PPD skin testing stimulates a T-lymphocyte driven type IV hypersensitivity reaction against subdermal tuberculin antigen. Causes of false positive PPD testing include prior vaccination with Bacillus Calmette-Guerin vaccine. This patient's positive test result likely represents a true positive. Her most significant risk factor for tuberculosis is volunteering in a skilled nursing care facility, as tuberculosis is more common and spreads more easily in crowded populations, such as in nursing facilities and prisons. Latent tuberculosis is treated with a prolonged course of isoniazid or rifampin.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, and E.</p><p>While overseas travel (Choice A) to endemic areas is a significant risk factor for tuberculosis, Ireland and western Europe are not endemic areas for tuberculosis. Endemic regions for tuberculosis include sub-Saharan Africa, the Indian subcontinent, and eastern and southeastern Asia.</p><p>Participation in high school events (Choice B) is not a significant risk factor for tuberculosis. Prolonged conditions of crowding, as in a prison, are a more significant risk factor for tuberculosis than single large events, which do not confer significant risk in non-endemic areas.</p><p>Raising livestock (Choice C) confers increased risk for acquiring particular infectious organisms, such as Bacillus anthracis, Coxiella burnetii, and Francisella tularensis. M. tuberculosis is not a zoonotic organism and is spread by human-to-human contact.</p><p>Sexual activity (Choice D) does not confer an increased risk for acquiring tuberculosis. M. tuberculosis is not a sexually transmitted organism and is spread by respiratory secretions.</p><p>Smoking marijuana (Choice E) does not confer an increased risk for acquiring tuberculosis. While chronic pulmonary disease, such as silicosis, does confer an increased risk for tuberculosis, this is unlikely in a teenage patient with occasional marijuana use.</p>",
"20": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pediatric HIV/AIDS infection should be suspected in patients who present with recurrent bacterial pneumonia or AIDS-defining illnesses such as disseminated mycobacterial and non-mycobacterial infection or Pneumocystis jiroveci infection.</p><p>HIV infection, acquired via vertical transmission from the patient's mother, is the most likely cause of the patient's recurrent pneumococcal pneumonia, otitis media, and herpes zoster infection with axillary lymphadenopathy. HIV transmission may occur at any time during gestation but is most commonly acquired at or around the time of delivery and in the immediate postpartum period during breastfeeding. Transmission is uncommon if the mother is taking antiretroviral therapy and has a decreased HIV viral load. As a result of screening for HIV in most developed countries, the incidence of perinatal and pediatric HIV infection has decreased, although this is not true around the world. While many pediatric patients with perinatally acquired HIV infection will manifest symptoms within the first few months to years, it has been recognized that a subpopulation of patients will not develop symptoms or AIDS-defining conditions until many years later, as in this case. Symptoms in children are non-specific, and AIDS-defining conditions in children younger than six vary slightly from those in older children and adults. Clues to the diagnosis in this case include the presence of nontuberculous mycobacterial infection in the patient's mother, which is an AIDS-defining illness and should prompt evaluation for HIV infection in this patient.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E. Chronic granulomatous disease (Choice A) is caused by NADPH oxidase deficiency and results in impaired intracellular killing of catalase positive pathogens. Streptococcus pneumoniae is catalase negative. Patients present with recurrent pyogenic infections with organisms such as S. aureus.</p><p>Hyperimmunoglobulinemia E syndrome (Choice C) is caused by mutations in the JAK-STAT signaling cascade, which impairs the development and function of T helper 17 (Th17) cells. Th17 cells secrete interleukin-17 (IL-17), a potent neutrophil chemotaxis agent, so deficiency results in poor neutrophil migration to sites of infection. Patients have recurrent skin infections but with non-inflamed abscesses (cold abscess) and diffuse atopic dermatitis.</p><p>Thymic-parathyroid dysplasia (DiGeorge syndrome) (Choice D) is caused by a 22q11 deletion that leads to failure of the third and fourth branchial pouches to develop. These structures give rise to the thymus and parathyroid glands, and their absence leads to thymic aplasia and hypocalcemia in cardiac and craniofacial defects. Patients are susceptible to viral and fungal infections.</p><p>X-linked agammaglobulinemia (Choice E) occurs as a result of mutations in the BTK gene that encodes a tyrosine kinase essential for all stages of B-lymphocyte development and proliferation. Patients lack functioning B lymphocytes, and therefore, lack normal concentrations of immunoglobulins. It nearly always presents within the first eighteen months of life with recurrent infections. It is more common in male patients given X-linked inheritance.</p>",
"21": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: E. histolytica is an amoebic parasite that causes intestinal amoebiasis, which presents with abdominal pain and bloody diarrhea. Extracolonic invasion of the bloodstream may occur, leading to the formation of hepatic abscesses that present with fever and right upper quadrant pain. Treatment requires combination therapy with metronidazole or tinidazole along with an anti-luminal agent such as paromomycin or iodoquinol.</p><p>Infection with Entamoeba histolytica leads to amoebiasis (amoebic dysentery). Amoebiasis is much more common in the developing world. E. histolytica is an amoebic parasite and forms cysts which can be transmitted through the fecal oral route and through ingestion of contaminated water. Amoebic cysts mature to trophozoites in the large bowel. Trophozoites may invade the colonic mucosa and interstitium, leading to symptoms of abdominal pain and bloody diarrhea. Invasive infections can lead to colonic perforation and sepsis. If trophozoites enter the bloodstream, they may travel to the liver and cause hepatic amoebic abscesses, which present with fever and right upper quadrant pain. Diagnosis can be made with identification of cysts on stool microscopy or peripheral smear, which may show trophozoites with endosomes containing red blood cells, as well as by antibody assay or polymerase chain reaction testing. Colonoscopy shows mucosal ulcers that are often flask shaped. Treatment requires combination therapy with metronidazole or tinidazole as well as an anti-luminal agent such as paromomycin or iodoquinol.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Giardia lamblia (Choice B) is a parasitic organism that causes giardiasis. Light microscopy shows a pear-shaped, binucleate, flagellated trophozoite. Parasitic overgrowth of the small bowel leads to intestinal malabsorption. While the diarrhea associated with giardiasis may occasionally be bloody, this symptom is less frequently observed than malabsorption.</p><p>Rotavirus (Choice C) is a double-stranded RNA virus that causes infectious diarrhea in children. The diarrhea caused by rotavirus is watery, profuse, and often foul-smelling; it is typically not bloody.</p><p>Salmonella typhi (Choice D) is a Gram-negative, encapsulated, facultative anaerobic bacillus that causes infectious diarrhea and typhoid fever, which presents with fever, abdominal pain, rash, and diarrhea. S. typhi does not typically cause focal right upper quadrant pain or bloody diarrhea.</p><p>Shigella sonnei (Choice E) is a Gram-negative bacillus that causes shigellosis, which presents with bloody diarrhea, and which may cause hemolytic uremic syndrome. S. sonnei does not cause focal right upper quadrant pain, which is suggestive of hepatic abscess.</p>",
"22": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Blunt kidney trauma is caused by rapid deceleration, such as a fall from a height. Evaluating for hematuria should be included in the initial trauma assessment and is the best indicator of kidney trauma.</p><p>This patient's fall from a height and subsequent left flank tenderness raises concern for blunt kidney trauma. Motor vehicle collisions, falls, and sports injuries are common causes of this type of injury. A rapid deceleration injury, such as caused by a fall, can cause the kidney to be crushed against the paravertebral muscles, avulsed from the ureteropelvic junction or the renal vessels, or thrombosed. In any of these cases, blood supply to the kidney is interrupted. Flank tenderness, flank bruising, or lower rib fractures should all raise concern for blunt kidney injury. Hematuria is a common presenting symptom seen in over two-thirds of patients with blunt renal injury. It should be investigated quickly with measurement of urine blood as part of the initial trauma assessment, as intravenous fluid administration can dilute hematuria and preclude detection.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Serum creatinine (Choice A) and urea nitrogen (Choice B) are not helpful in diagnosing acute blunt kidney trauma. Any abnormality in renal function caused by trauma is masked by the contralateral functioning kidney. Therefore, neither creatinine nor urea nitrogen may be used as an indicator of renal trauma.</p><p>Urine protein (Choice D) and volume (Choice E) may also be normal after blunt kidney trauma. They are not helpful in identifying the presence or absence of traumatic kidney injury. These values may later change if the filtering capacity of the kidney is impaired, but they are not useful in the initial trauma assessment.</p>",
"23": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pertussis is caused by infection with B. pertussis and presents with episodes of paroxysmal coughing sometimes followed by post-tussive syncope or emesis. Symptoms persist for weeks to months, and while treatment with antibiotics can shorten the duration of symptoms, the most appropriate intervention is the prevention of infection. The bacteria can be transmitted in respiratory droplets, thus droplet precautions, including the wearing of a mask and eye protection, should be instituted immediately.</p><p>Droplet isolation or droplet precautions, in which the eyes, nose, and mouth are covered by glasses and a mask, would be the most effective way to prevent nosocomial spread of Bordetella pertussis, an infection characterized by episodes of severe paroxysmal coughing and post-tussive emesis. Pertussis classically presents in three stages. The catarrhal stage is first, which presents with fever, rhinorrhea, and a mild cough. This progresses to the paroxysmal stage after 1 to 2 weeks, in which patients experience violent bouts of extreme coughing, at times violent enough to cause secondary traumatic injury such as rib fractures, urinary incontinence, pneumothorax, and facial petechiae. Often, such coughing fits are accompanied by cyanosis and may be followed by syncope, emesis, or apnea. The paroxysmal stage can last for weeks to months. Symptoms resolve during the convalescent stage, which lasts from 1 to 4 weeks. Pertussis can be prevented with immunization in the form of acellular pertussis, typically administered along with tetanus and diphtheria vaccines (DTaP). It can be transmitted in respiratory droplets, thus all individuals who come into contact with infected patients should wear a mask and eye protection.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, E, F, G, H, and I.</p><p>Antiviral therapy for all health care workers (Choice A) can be given with oseltamivir in an influenza outbreak, but B. pertussis is a bacterial infection and would not be treated with an antiviral medication.</p><p>Contact isolation (Choice B) is reserved for pathogens such as Clostridium difficile, methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, and others that are easily transmitted through contact with the skin. These precautions involve the wearing of disposable gloves and gowns.</p><p>Erythromycin therapy for all healthcare workers (Choice D) could be considered in instances of a B. pertussis outbreak, which can occur if the index case is not recognized or droplet precautions are not instituted but is not necessary in this case.</p><p>Intravenous immune globulin therapy for all health care workers (Choice E) is not used for post-exposure prophylaxis for B. pertussis.</p><p>Intravenous immune globulin therapy for patients (Choice F) is occasionally given after exposure to certain pathogens. For example, those exposed to rabies are given both rabies immune globulin and a rabies vaccine.</p><p>Pertussis vaccination for all healthcare workers (Choice G) is not correct. While all adults should receive the tetanus, diphtheria and acellular pertussis vaccine (Tdap) every 10 years, infection can occur even in patients who have recently been vaccinated. The best method of preventing infection is the use of droplet precautions.</p><p>Pertussis vaccination for the patient (Choice H) will not change her disease course, although vaccination is still recommended at 4 months, 6 months, 15 months, and at age 4.</p><p>No intervention (Choice I) is not appropriate as failure to contain this infection could lead to exposure of many other patients and staff.</p>",
"24": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: When a fracture is suspected, the next step in management is radiographic assessment of the affected area and the joints above and below it.</p><p>This patient's presentation with distal thigh swelling is concerning for a fracture of the diaphysis of the femur. Femoral fractures are a common long bone fracture in children, with peaks of incidence in toddlers because of accidental trauma and adolescents because of motor vehicle collisions. A femoral shaft fracture may be complicated by shortening of the muscles of the knee and hip which causes the fractured femur to displace, so early recognition and intervention is needed to prevent permanent limb length discrepancy. Clinically, most femoral shaft fractures will demonstrate obvious deformity, swelling, shortening, or crepitus on examination. A neurovascular examination distal to the site of swelling should be performed to ensure that the fracture has not caused damage to nerves or arteries. The next step in management of a suspected femoral fracture is radiographic assessment using standard anterior-posterior and lateral plain x-rays of the affected area and the joints above and below the suspected fracture. This ensures correct diagnosis and assists in management planning.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, D, and E.</p><p>Reassurance (Choice A) and application of local heat (Choice B) are not appropriate in this case as the suspected femoral fracture will need to be immobilized and may require operative intervention. In cases of mild musculoskeletal injuries that do not require medical evaluation, application of ice is more effective than application of heat.</p><p>Range of motion exercises (Choice D) may be needed following complete healing of the femoral fracture, but if performed now without adequate treatment, they would likely worsen the fracture. Immobilization is an important step in treatment of a femoral fracture.</p><p>Acetaminophen therapy (Choice E) may be used for mild musculoskeletal complaints and is safe for use in children. However, treating pain without addressing the underlying cause, such as a fracture, is not appropriate.</p>",
"25": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Polycystic ovary syndrome presents with menstrual irregularities, signs of androgen excess, obesity, insulin resistance, and polycystic ovaries on ultrasound. Treatment includes weight loss and combined oral contraceptives for menstrual regulation, and antihyperglycemic medications or insulin for the management of diabetes mellitus, if diagnosed.</p><p>Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) presents with irregular menses, infertility, hirsutism, and acne, often in patients with an increased body weight. It requires two of the following for diagnosis: oligo- or anovulation, polycystic ovaries on ultrasound, and clinical or biochemical signs of hyperandrogenism (eg, hirsutism, acne). It is also associated with signs of insulin resistance, such as acanthosis nigricans and increased blood glucose concentrations. PCOS is caused by an abnormal hypothalamic hormonal feedback response, that is caused by a primary functional ovarian hyperandrogenism (increased androgens produced by the theca lutein cells), which leads to increased luteinizing hormone (LH) in comparison to follicle stimulating hormone (FSH). Treatment includes weight loss, combined oral contraceptives for menstrual regulation, and spironolactone or ketoconazole for hirsutism. Clomiphene stimulates increased ovulation and can also be utilized for individuals desiring pregnancy as can metformin. If the patient is diagnosed with diabetes mellitus, oral antihyperglycemic medications or insulin may also be necessary.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</p><p>Hypothalamus (Choice A) pathologies can lead to hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, which can lead to primary or secondary amenorrhea caused by anovulation. Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism is more commonly seen in young, athletic, thin female patients and would be less likely in this obese patient with hirsutism.</p><p>Pancreas (Choice C) pathologies include insulinomas and glucagonomas. Insulinomas are tumors of the pancreatic β cells that present with hypoglycemia, whereas glucagonomas are tumors of the α cells that present with increased blood glucose concentrations, polyuria, polydipsia, and weight loss. Neither present with hirsutism.</p><p>Pituitary gland (Choice D) pathologies can also lead to hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, which presents with anovulation and menstrual irregularities, as well as deficiencies in growth hormone, leading to short stature, and prolactin.</p><p>Thyroid gland (Choice E) pathologies include hypo- and hyperthyroidism, both of which can present with anovulation and menstrual irregularities. Hyperthyroidism presents with heat intolerance, palpitations, weight loss, diaphoresis, and diarrhea. Hypothyroidism presents with weight gain, fatigue, constipation, and cold intolerance. This patient does not have any symptoms to suggest a thyroid pathology.</p>",
"26": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Constitutional growth delay is a normal growth variant that occurs in patients with parents of normal stature. Patients typically present with short stature, delayed pubertal development, slow but consistent growth velocity, and a bone age less than chronological age. It is associated with normal development and stature once the patient reaches puberty.</p><p>This patient's most likely diagnosis is constitutional growth delay, which is a normal variant of growth. Short stature has a variety of causes, such as constitutional growth delay, familial (hereditary) short stature, chronic steroid use, endocrine abnormalities (eg, hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency), cardiopulmonary disease, and genetic disorders (eg, Down syndrome, Turner syndrome). Familial short stature and constitutional growth delay are the most common causes of short stature. Familial short stature is associated with short stature in both parents, a normal gain of height velocity, and a bone age that corresponds with chronological age, whereas a constitutional growth delay is independent of parental stature, has a consistently decreased growth velocity, and has a bone age younger than chronological age. Patients with constitutional growth delay may experience a delayed onset of puberty. These patients typically reach a normal adult height, with more rapid increases in height gain velocity as they progress through puberty. This patient has parents with normal stature and a decreased growth velocity, leading to a height that is consistently in the 3rd percentile. He does not have other signs or symptoms to suggest a pathologic cause of short stature, making constitutional growth delay the most likely diagnosis.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</p><p>Adrenal insufficiency (Choice A) typically presents with fatigue, nausea, fasting hypoglycemia, muscle weakness, and morning headache caused by glucocorticoid insufficiency. Patients with primary adrenal insufficiency may also demonstrate mineralocorticoid and androgen deficiency, which would present with urinary salt wasting and decreased body hair, respectively. This patient does not demonstrate the symptoms or physical examination signs of adrenal insufficiency.</p><p>Familial short stature (Choice C) typically presents in young patients whose parents are both of short stature with a normal growth velocity and bone age consistent with chronological age. These patients will remain of short stature into adulthood. This patient has parents with normal stature, making this answer less likely.</p><p>Growth hormone deficiency (Choice D) is a rare condition that can present in infancy or childhood depending on the severity of deficiency. Acquired growth hormone deficiency is typically caused by a non-functional pituitary tumor and presents with severe growth delay and a bone age younger than chronological age. It is associated with a rapid decline in the height-for-age curve, crossing percentiles. This patient's growth velocity has been slow but consistent, making growth hormone deficiency a less likely cause of his short stature.</p><p>Insufficient caloric intake (Choice E) typically results in an abnormally low weight along with signs of malnutrition such as dry skin and hair loss (caused by decreased protein stores). This patient has a normal weight and lacks physical examination signs of malnutrition.</p>",
"27": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Duchenne muscular dystrophy, an X-linked recessive condition, results in progressive proximal muscle weakness after a period of relatively normal development. Symptoms include an inability to rise from a squatting position and weakness of the lower limb girdle. Late complications include dilated cardiomyopathy and respiratory insufficiency.</p><p>X-linked recessive is the mode of inheritance for Duchenne muscular dystrophy. Children with Duchenne muscular dystrophy typically have an unremarkable birth history, then begin to demonstrate signs of proximal muscle weakness early in childhood. Physical examination discloses difficulty rising from squatting position, calf pseudohypertrophy, and a Gower sign (using hands to \"climb\" up the lower extremities when rising from the floor). Proximal muscles include those of the shoulder and hip girdles, such as the adductors and flexors of the hip and thigh. Muscle biopsy used to be a mainstay of diagnosis, but genetic testing has rendered this largely unnecessary. Most affected patients will require the use of a wheelchair by early adolescence. Additional late findings include dilated cardiomyopathy and respiratory failure, which may be the cause of death in these patients.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Autosomal dominant (Choice A) and autosomal recessive (Choice B) are not the inheritance patterns of Duchenne muscular dystrophy, although either one can be the inheritance pattern of Limb-Girdle muscular dystrophy, which is characterized by shoulder and pelvic girdle weakness with normal distal muscle strength.</p><p>X-linked dominant (Choice C) is the inheritance pattern of scapuloperoneal myopathy, which presents with weakness of the distal leg and scapula. Facial muscles are occasionally affected. X-linked dominant differs from X-linked recessive in that only a single copy is required for expression of the disease phenotype, even in females, who have two X-chromosomes.</p><p>No known mendelian pattern (Choice E) is not the correct answer. Many of the muscular dystrophies are inherited in a mendelian pattern, although inflammatory and infectious myopathies occur sporadically and are not genetically determined.</p>",
"28": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: In the fetal circulation, increased PVR prevents a left-to-right shunt from the LV to the RV through a VSD. At the time of birth, the rapid decrease in PVR creates a substantial pressure gradient between the two ventricles and allows for the development of a left-to-right shunt. The PVR continues to drop over the following weeks and likely accounts for the change in this patient's murmur.</p><p>Decreased pulmonary vascular resistance most likely explains the increased intensity of this patient's holosystolic murmur, which is consistent with a ventricular septal defect (VSD). Normal fetal circulation is unique in that oxygenated blood arrives from the maternal circulation via the umbilical vein and reaches the inferior vena cava. From there, oxygenated blood is shunted from the right atrium to the left atrium, left ventricle {LV), and systemic circulation. The fetal lungs are filled with amniotic fluid while in utero, so the pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) is high. In patients with a VSD, increased PVR effectively prevents left-to-right shunting of blood from the LV to the right ventricle (RV). At birth, amniotic fluid is cleared and expelled from the lungs and the PVR decreases dramatically, followed by a gradual decrease in PVR over the next several weeks. As the PVR decreases, the pressure gradient between the LV and the RV increases such that blood is shunted from the LV to the RV with each cardiac cycle. This change in PVR explains the change in the patient's murmur.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</p><p>Closed ductus arteriosus (DA) (Choice A) is a normal finding in newborns and occurs within several days of delivery. Continued patency of the DA leads to a continuous murmur, not a holosystolic murmur.</p><p>Improved atrial natriuretic factor functioning (Choice C) is not the answer. This is a substance that is released by atria in response to increases in circulating volume to induce natriuresis in the kidneys. It does not explain the change in this patient's murmur.</p><p>Improved myocardial contractility (Choice D) would not explain the change in the murmur as increased contractility would likely be equal between the two ventricles, with little to no change in the pressure gradient between the two.</p><p>Increased circulating blood volume (Choice E) is incorrect. VSDs that are large enough to cause right ventricular overload may manifest later in life with evidence of right heart failure and volume overload.</p>",
"29": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objectives: Minimal change disease is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children and can be idiopathic or triggered by a recent infection or immune stimulus. Patients classically present with dependent edema in the buttocks, lower back, and legs, foamy or dark-colored urine, hypoalbuminemia, and proteinuria.</p><p>Nephrotic syndrome is characterized by edema secondary to excessive proteinuria resulting in hypoalbuminemia and diminished intravascular oncotic pressure. This occurs because of loss of normal size and charge filtration capability of the glomerular capillary and Bowman capsule interface. Causes of nephrotic syndrome include minimal change disease, membranous nephropathy, and focal segmental glomerulosclerosis. Minimal change disease is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children and can be idiopathic or triggered by a recent infection or immune stimulus. Patients classically present with dependent edema in the buttocks, lower back, and legs, foamy or dark-colored urine, hypoalbuminemia, and proteinuria. Complications include hypercoagulability caused by urinary loss of antithrombin-3 and infection caused by urinary loss of γ-globulins. The loss of proteins and decreased serum oncotic pressure in nephrotic syndrome leads to hepatic protein synthesis, especially of lipoproteins, which can contribute to hyperlipidemia.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis (Choice A) is a type of nephritic syndrome characterized by dark colored urine, hypertension, hematuria, and proteinuria following an infection with group A streptococci. Minimal change disease is a nephrotic syndrome, characterized by massive proteinuria without hematuria.</p><p>Hemolytic-uremic syndrome (Choice B) is typically associated with infection from enterohemorrhagic Escherichia coli O157:H7. It classically presents in children with bloody diarrhea accompanied by microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute renal failure.</p><p>Henoch-Schonlein purpura (Choice C) is a small-vessel vasculitis that occurs in children and typically presents with gravity-dependent purpura (eg, buttocks, legs, and feet), arthralgias, hematuria, and abdominal pain (eg, intussusception).</p><p>Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS) (Choice E) is a nephrotic syndrome that can result in proteinuria and pitting edema. However, FSGS is commonly associated with sickle cell disease, opioid abuse, and HIV infection, and is characterized by segmental sclerosis of the glomeruli.</p>",
"30": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pertussis occurs because of an infection from B. pertussis and may lead to severe paroxysmal coughing that may be accompanied by cyanosis and followed by syncope, emesis, or apnea. Pertussis can be prevented by immunization with the diphtheria-tetanus-acellular pertussis vaccine, which is given as a multiple dose series beginning at age two months.</p><p>Episodes of severe paroxysmal coughing are concerning for pertussis. Pertussis occurs because of an infection from Bordetella pertussis and classically presents in three stages. The catarrhal stage is first, which presents with fever, rhinorrhea, and a mild cough. This progresses to the paroxysmal stage after one to two weeks, in which patients experience violent bouts of extreme coughing, at times violent enough to cause secondary traumatic injury such as rib fractures, urinary incontinence, and pneumothorax. Often, such coughing fits are accompanied by cyanosis and may be followed by syncope, emesis, or apnea. The paroxysmal stage can last for weeks to months. Symptoms resolve during the convalescent stage, which lasts from one to four weeks. Pertussis toxin causes impaired recruitment and migration of phagocytes and production of inflammatory mediators. Patients often have significant lymphocytosis. Pertussis can be prevented with immunization in the form of acellular pertussis, typically administered along with tetanus and diphtheria vaccines. The Centers for Disease Control (CDC) recommends administering the vaccine in a multiple dose series beginning at two months of age. Pertussis is treated with antibiotics (typically macrolides) and supportive care.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Haemophilus influenzae type b-protein conjugate (Choice B) vaccine uses capsular polysaccharide in conjugation with toxoid protein for prevention of infections caused by H. influenzae type b. H. influenzae is a Gram-negative bacterium that can result in various mucosal infections such as conjunctivitis, otitis media, bronchitis, and pneumonia. It was the leading cause of epiglottitis in children prior to widespread immunization. The CDC recommends administering H. influenzae type b-protein conjugate in a four-dose series beginning at age two months.</p><p>Influenza virus (Choice C) vaccine prevents infection by influenza virus, an orthomyxovirus which is transmitted via respiratory droplets and causes seasonal pandemics. Symptoms include fever, chills, myalgias, malaise, headaches, nausea, and vomiting. Pneumonia may develop. Influenza does not typically cause paroxysmal coughing. The CDC recommends annual influenza virus vaccination beginning at age greater than six months.</p><p>Measles-mumps-rubella (Choice D) vaccine prevents measles, mumps, and rubella infection, which can cause different viral syndromes. None of these viruses cause paroxysmal coughing. The CDC recommends vaccination with measles-mumps-rubella in a two-dose series beginning at age one year.</p><p>Pneumococcal (Choice E) vaccine prevents infections with Streptococcus pneumoniae, which can cause pneumonia and meningitis. It does not cause paroxysmal coughing. The CDC recommends vaccination with pneumococcal vaccine in a four-dose series beginning at age two months.</p>",
"31": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Intestinal malrotation is a congenital anomaly in which the midgut incompletely rotates during development, leaving the midgut attached to a thin stalk of mesentery at the base of the SMA, instead of the normally broad-based mesenteric stalk. It can be recognized on imaging by the presence of small intestine in the right abdomen only. Malrotation increases the risk for volvulus of the small bowel at the base of the SMA (midgut volvulus), which causes small bowel obstruction.</p><p>Intestinal malrotation is a congenital anomaly in which the midgut incompletely rotates during development. This leaves the midgut attached to a thin stalk of mesentery at the base of the superior mesenteric artery (SMA), instead of the normally broad-based mesenteric stalk. The cecum also remains in an abnormal position within the mid-upper abdomen instead of the right lower quadrant and is attached to the lateral abdominal wall by fibrous bands, which cross over the top of the duodenum and can also cause constriction. This leaves the small intestine at risk for volvulus originating at the base of the SMA. While most patients present in the first year of life, some individuals do not present until later childhood or adulthood. In this case, the patient's upper gastrointestinal barium series demonstrates the presence of the small intestine only within the right abdomen. In its usual anatomic position, the duodenum crosses the midline toward the ligament of Treitz and the remaining small intestine courses throughout the abdominal quadrants. Thus, these imaging findings suggest malrotation of the small intestine. Malrotation of the intestines predisposes to intestinal obstruction. In this case, the persistent vomiting of bilious emesis suggests a partial small bowel obstruction secondary to the patient's malrotation.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Cyclic vomiting syndrome (Choice A) is an idiopathic disorder seen in both children and adults characterized by recurrent episodes of vomiting with periods of normal health between episodes. In this case, the bilious nature of the vomit suggests that it is attributable to a structural cause instead.</p><p>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) (Choice B) is caused by acid reflux into the lower esophagus. This commonly occurs as a result of increased intra-abdominal pressure or diminished closing pressure of the lower esophageal sphincter permitting acidic contents of the stomach to reflux into the esophagus. In an infant, gastroesophageal reflux disease presents with frequent regurgitation without excessive irritability. Bilious vomiting is a warning sign of a structural cause of vomiting rather than GERD.</p><p>Hiatal hernia (Choice C) is caused by a defect in the diaphragm at the esophageal hiatus that allows the lower esophageal sphincter and variable amounts of the stomach to be displaced into the thorax. It can cause heartburn, nausea or emesis, dysphagia, regurgitation, and shortness of breath. It does not cause bilious emesis or the radiographic findings seen in this case.</p><p>Pyloric stenosis (Choice E) is caused by hypertrophy of the pyloric sphincter, leading to gastric outlet obstruction and vomiting. It commonly presents with nonbloody, nonbilious emesis after feedings, and begins around three to six weeks of age. It is more common in male infants. Symptoms may begin with occasional vomiting, then progress to projectile emesis after every feeding, dehydration, and malnutrition as the stenosis worsens.</p>",
"32": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Chronic lymphocytic (Hashimoto) thyroiditis is the most common form of thyroiditis and is characterized by the presence of antithyroid peroxidase and antithyroglobulin antibodies. Patients with chronic disease present with signs and symptoms of hypothyroidism, including fatigue, cold intolerance, weight gain, hyporeflexia, myxedema, and dry, cool skin. Patients in the acute stage of the disease may experience mild neck discomfort, although the thyroid is not usually tender.</p><p>Chronic lymphocytic (Hashimoto) thyroiditis is the most common form of thyroiditis and is characterized by the presence of antithyroid peroxidase and antithyroglobulin antibodies. Female patients are disproportionately affected compared to male patients. The early stages of Hashimoto thyroiditis can present with neck swelling and a diffusely enlarged, nontender thyroid gland. The patient may display evidence of hyperthyroidism from destruction of thyroid follicular cells and the unregulated release of thyroid hormone. During the chronic stages of Hashimoto thyroiditis, the patient typically develops primary hypothyroidism from the underlying inflammatory destruction of normal thyroid parenchyma and resultant insufficient production of thyroid hormone. Signs and symptoms during this stage include fatigue, cold intolerance, weight gain, hyporeflexia, myxedema, and dry, cool skin. Fine needle biopsy demonstrates lymphoid infiltrate with the presence of follicles and Hürthle cells. Treatment is with oral replacement of thyroid hormone.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, C, D, and E.</p><p>Acute suppurative thyroiditis (Choice A) presents with rapid onset of neck pain, tenderness, erythema, and warmth to touch, as well as symptoms of hyperthyroidism secondary to destruction of thyroid follicular cells. Commonly implicated organisms include Staphylococcus and Streptococcus species.</p><p>Endemic goiter (Choice C) is caused by dietary iodine deficiency. It is rare in developed countries because of the routine fortification of common food staples, such as salt, with iodine.</p><p>Graves disease (Choice D) is the most common cause of hyperthyroidism in children and adolescents, as well as in adults, and it is caused by an autoantibody that activates thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) receptors on the thyroid. It presents with symptoms of hyperthyroidism, pretibial myxedema, and thyroid ophthalmopathy, which can cause diplopia, proptosis, and restrictive strabismus. Serum concentrations of TSH are typically reduced and antithyroid peroxidase antibodies would not be present.</p><p>Thyroid carcinoma (Choice E) is typically non-functional and does not secrete thyroid hormone. Papillary thyroid carcinoma is encountered most frequently.</p>",
"33": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: AI results in an increased LV end-diastolic volume as regurgitant blood from the aorta enters the LV during diastole, which can be auscultated as a diastolic murmur. The retrograde flow of blood during diastole results in a lower diastolic pressure, which is reflected as an increased pulse pressure.</p><p>Left ventricular volume overload as a result of aortic insufficiency (AI) explains this patient's widened pulse pressure, blowing diastolic murmur, and bounding radial pulses in addition to his exertional symptoms. The causes of AI, also called aortic regurgitation, include bicuspid aortic valve, aortic root dilation, and a history of rheumatic heart disease. It is also commonly associated with syndromes such as Marfan and Turner syndrome. AI results from an incompetent aortic valve that allows retrograde blood flow from the aortic root to the left ventricle (LV) during diastole. This results in an increased LV end-diastolic volume (EDV) as both blood from the aorta and the left atrium fill the LV. Increased volume stretches the compliant ventricle thereby increasing myocardial contractility, which is reflected as an increased stroke volume (SV) and a comparatively increased cardiac output. Over time, eccentric hypertrophy of the LV leads to loss of normal compliance and progressive LV systolic dysfunction.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Increased pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) (Choice A) is not a feature of isolated AI. Increased PVR occurs in conditions such as pulmonary hypertension or pulmonic stenosis. Eventually, left-sided heart failure can lead to increased PVR, but this occurs over time and the patient in this case shows no symptoms of pulmonary hypertension or cor pulmonale.</p><p>Increased systemic cardiac output (Choice B) is incorrect. In compensated AI, the cardiac output is maintained, but over time the progressive hypertrophy of the LV in combination with ongoing aortic regurgitation leads to systolic dysfunction and decreased cardiac output.</p><p>Increased systemic vascular resistance (SVR) (Choice C) is incorrect. SVR tends to be relatively normal in individuals with chronic compensated AI, although they tend to have a greater disparity between systolic and diastolic pressures. The large volume ejected during systole transiently increases the systolic pressure while the regurgitant volume back into the LV leads to a lower than expected diastolic pressure.</p><p>Left ventricular pressure overload (Choice D) is incorrect. In young patients with chronic AI and no evidence of heart failure, the LV is able to compensate for increased end-diastolic volume by distending, thereby maintaining a normal LV end-diastolic pressure. Over time, however, eccentric hypertrophy can cause diastolic dysfunction and increased LV pressure.</p>",
"34": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: A decreased velocity of weight gain compared to growth in length raises the concern for poor weight gain and malnutrition. A diet diary with calorie counts will investigate whether inadequate caloric intake is the cause of the child's poor weight gain.</p><p>This child demonstrates a decreased velocity of weight gain compared to his growth in length, raising the concern for a diagnosis of poor weight gain and malnutrition. Poor weight gain is a common diagnosis seen in primary care offices in approximately 5% of pediatric patients. Risk factors include prematurity, developmental delay, congenital abnormalities, or any condition resulting in inadequate intake, absorption, or metabolism of nutrients. Poverty, social isolation, certain nutritional beliefs and practices, poor parenting skills, substance abuse, violence, or other abuse also predispose to poor weight gain. The first step in evaluation of poor weight gain is to take a thorough history of dietary practices and review of systems. If no suspected underlying organic cause is found, management of mild malnutrition should involve parental education and dietary advice, changes to dietary practices, provision of home-based support and referral to community or governmental programs to increase access to food. Recommending a diet diary with calorie counts will both investigate whether inadequate caloric intake is the cause of the child's poor weight gain and also assist caregivers in creating a personalized feeding plan.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Reevaluating weight in 1 week (Choice B) without an associated intervention will not likely improve this patient's weight gain. After initiating a dietary plan, close follow up is warranted, but this alone is insufficient.</p><p>Test the stool for reducing substances and fecal fat (Choice C) may be performed in children whose poor weight gain has not resolved with appropriate dietary intervention to investigate malabsorption as a potential cause. It is not warranted prior to attempting dietary intervention in an otherwise asymptomatic child.</p><p>Admit to the hospital for observation (Choice D) is not necessary at this time as conservative measures including dietary changes have not yet been tried. There is no reason to suspect that this weight loss is caused by caregiver neglect or non-accidental trauma, in which case admission would be appropriate.</p><p>Obtain an x-ray of the abdomen (Choice E) is not necessary in this case as the patient has no symptoms, such as vomiting, abdominal pain, decreased appetite, or diarrhea, suggestive of an abdominal pathology.</p>",
"35": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is a type of nephritic syndrome characterized by dark colored urine, hypertension, hematuria, and proteinuria following an infection with group A streptococcus. Hypertension associated with poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is caused by salt and fluid retention.</p><p>Acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is a type of nephritic syndrome characterized by dark colored urine, hypertension, acute kidney injury, hematuria, and proteinuria following an infection with group A streptococcus. Glomerular injury in poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis involves immune complex formation in the glomerulus, and light microscopy shows deposits of C3 and IgG in the glomerulus and mesangium. Urinalysis shows hematuria and variable proteinuria, with or without red blood cell casts. Hypertension associated with poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is caused by salt and fluid retention resulting in volume overload. Fluid retention can lead to generalized edema including periorbital, pretibial, and pedal edema. It can also lead to complications involving heart failure, pulmonary edema, and respiratory distress.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Catecholamine excess (Choice A) can occur in pheochromocytoma, which presents with episodic headaches, sweating, palpitations, tremors, agitation, tachycardia, tachypnea, and treatment-refractory hypertension. It is the most common primary tumor of the adrenal medulla, and is marked by the functional secretion of epinephrine, norepinephrine, and dopamine, which cause episodic hyperadrenergic symptoms.</p><p>Idiopathic hypertension (Choice B) would be the diagnosis if there was no preceding cause of hypertension, but this patient presents with a nephritic syndrome caused by poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis as a cause of hypertension.</p><p>Mineralocorticoid excess (Choice C) can occur in hyperaldosteronism and increased secretion of aldosterone from the adrenal gland. This can be seen with an adrenal adenoma or adrenal hyperplasia and presents with hypertension, normal or decreased potassium concentrations, and metabolic alkalosis. It would not present with hematuria and proteinuria.</p><p>Stimulation of the parasympathetic system (Choice D) can occur with cholinergic toxicity and would lead to excessive salivation, lacrimation, urination, defecation, altered mental status, and miosis. It would be unlikely to cause hypertension. Increased sympathetic, not parasympathetic, system activation can lead to hypertension.</p>",
"36": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Congenital megacolon (Hirschsprung disease) is caused by the congenital absence of the distal portion of the myenteric plexus, which causes failure to pass meconium and constipation. It is associated with Down syndrome. An explosive expulsion of feces on rectal examination is a common clinical finding, and a barium enema is used to evaluate for the condition.</p><p>Congenital megacolon (also known as Hirschsprung disease or intestinal aganglionosis) is caused by the congenital absence of the distal portion of the myenteric plexus, a part of the enteric nervous system located between the inner and outer layers of the muscularis externa in the bowel wall. During development, this plexus migrates cephalad to caudal and incomplete migration results in absence of the plexus in the distal portion of the gastrointestinal tract. This often leads to an inability to pass meconium within the first few days of life and, eventually, large bowel obstruction. An explosive expulsion of feces on rectal examination is a common clinical finding. Barium enema is used to evaluate for congenital megacolon in children. Congenital megacolon is associated with Down syndrome. Patients with Down syndrome are also at increased risk for dementia (Alzheimer type), acute lymphoblastic leukemia, acute myeloid leukemia, cardiac septal defects, and duodenal atresia.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Dilation of lymphatic vessels of the bowel (Choice B) caused by obstruction is found in intestinal lymphangiectasia. Loss of lymphocytes into the gut secondary to this dilation can lead to lymphopenia and subsequent immunosuppression. It does not contribute to failure to pass meconium after birth.</p><p>Duodenal atresia (Choice C) is caused by failure of re-canalization of duodenal lumen. Duodenal atresia presents with vomiting and abdominal distension, as well as distension of the stomach and duodenum on abdominal x-ray. Like congenital megacolon, duodenal atresia is more common in children with Down syndrome.</p><p>Pancreatic enzyme insufficiency (Choice D) is seen in cystic fibrosis because of destruction of the pancreatic duct. The absence of the digestive enzymes made by the pancreas leads to malnutrition (eg, cachexia, muscle wasting), weight loss, vitamin deficiencies, and frequent, loose, foul-smelling, sometimes oily stools (steatorrhea). Cystic fibrosis may also present with meconium ileus, another cause of failure to pass stool after birth. However, this patient's presentation is more characteristic of congenital megacolon.</p><p>Villous atrophy (Choice E) in the small bowel is a characteristic finding of Celiac disease. Celiac disease is a gluten-sensitive enteropathy which causes inflammatory infiltration of the proximal small bowel, which leads to characteristic intraepithelial lymphocytosis, villous atrophy, and crypt hyperplasia. Malabsorption of substances in the proximal small bowel ensues, which presents symptomatically as chronic diarrhea, steatorrhea, and nutritional deficiencies.</p>",
"37": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Autosomal dominant mutations in ankyrin, spectrin, and band erythrocyte membrane proteins cause hereditary spherocytosis through loss of the normal connection between the inner membrane skeleton and the outer lipid bilayer. Clinical symptoms include jaundice, splenomegaly, and anemia. Peripheral smear shows normocytic, normochromic anemia with the presence of spherocytes. Definitive treatment is with splenectomy. r \" ,</p><p>Normocytic and normochromic is the correct answer. This patient with evidence of hemolytic anemia, increased reticulocyte count, and a family history of similar symptoms requiring splenectomy likely has hereditary spherocytosis (HS). HS can occur if there are mutations in one or more proteins that link the erythrocyte inner membrane skeleton to the outer lipid bilayer, and include mutations in spectrin, ankyrin, band 3, and band 4.2. These mutations are inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern. Spherocytosis results from progressive loss of the erythrocyte membrane over time and gives the erythrocytes a spherical shape; the surface area decreases but the volume of the cell remains unchanged. This impairs the erythrocyte's ability to conform in small vessels of the microcirculation, especially within the spleen, where spherocytes accumulate. Patients often present with splenomegaly, hemolytic anemia, and pigmented gallstones. Peripheral smear will show normocytic, normochromic erythrocytes and, if there is a high degree of hemolysis, reticulocytes, which appear as larger, pale blue erythrocytes. Definitive management involves splenectomy.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Macrocytic and hyperchromic (Choice A) erythrocytes are seen in vitamin B12 or folate deficiency, which may occur in pediatric patients with malabsorptive conditions such as celiac disease. In older patients and adults, it can be seen in gastric bypass, chronic alcohol use, and pernicious anemia.</p><p>Macrocytic and hypochromic (Choice B) erythrocytes may result from a combination of diseases such as vitamin B12 deficiency, folate deficiency (macrocytosis), iron deficiency, anemia of chronic disease, or thalassemia (hypochromia).</p><p>Microcytic and hypochromic (Choice C) erythrocytes are seen in iron deficiency, which can result from chronic blood loss. It would be uncommon in such a young patient without evidence of chronic gastrointestinal bleeding.</p><p>Normocytic and hypochromic (Choice D) erythrocytes are commonly seen in thalassemia. Additional findings of peripheral smear include target cells.</p>",
"38": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Maternal diabetes mellitus exposes a fetus to increased concentrations of blood glucose, in response to which the fetus increases production of insulin and decreases gluconeogenesis. Following birth, this can lead to postnatal hypoglycemia as the newborn is no longer exposed to increased concentrations of blood glucose in the maternal circulation. This presents with tremors, diaphoresis, irritability, lethargy, and seizures.</p><p>Excessive fetal exposure to insulin in utero can cause macrosomia, neonatal respiratory distress syndrome or hyaline membrane disease, and hypoglycemia in the newborn. Human placental lactogen produced by the placenta increases maternal insulin resistance. This leads to increased concentrations of glucose in the maternal circulation, and subsequently, the fetal circulation. Fetal hyperglycemia in turn causes the fetus to produce excess insulin and decrease the rate of gluconeogenesis. The insulin then acts as a growth factor, leading to macrosomia (as in this patient), and it inhibits the production of surfactant, which can lead to respiratory distress syndrome. After birth, the newborn continues to synthesize insulin but is no longer exposed to maternal blood glucose which leads to hypoglycemia. Hypoglycemia presents in the newborn with tremors, diaphoresis, irritability, lethargy, and seizures. Treatment is supportive and includes administration of supplemental glucose while neonatal endocrine regulation self-corrects. Maternal diabetes mellitus, which is usually diagnosed by a routine glucose tolerance test at 24 to 28 weeks' gestation, can also cause other neonatal complications including congenital cardiac and neurologic abnormalities (eg, patent ductus arteriosus, ventricular septal defect, caudal regression), preterm delivery, polycythemia, and hyperbilirubinemia.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Adrenal insufficiency (Choice A) causes decreased serum cortisol and presents with fatigue, hypotension, hypoglycemia, and abdominal pain. In children, it is commonly caused by congenital adrenal hyperplasia, which leads to varying concentrations of mineralocorticoids, glucocorticoids, and androgens depending on the enzyme affected. In general, it presents with ambiguous genitalia or precocious puberty and can present with salt wasting in infancy. This patient has a normal genital examination, making this less likely.</p><p>Hypermagnesemia (Choice B) in infants can be caused by magnesium sulfate administration in the mother in preeclampsia or eclampsia. It causes hypotonia and respiratory depression in the newborn but does not cause jitteriness.</p><p>Hypocalcemia (Choice C) presents with jitteriness, myoclonus, and seizures, as well as occasional laryngospasm or bronchospasm. It is commonly associated with DiGeorge syndrome, which causes parathyroid hypoplasia, and resultant severe hypocalcemia. However, DiGeorge syndrome is associated with craniofacial abnormalities that this patient does not exhibit.</p><p>Hypothyroidism (Choice E) presents in the first few months of life with feeding difficulties, lethargy, constipation, myxedema, macroglossia, large fontanelles, hypotonia, and hypothermia. It is not generally apparent at birth.</p>",
"39": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hemophilia A is an X-linked recessive disease resulting in deficiency or absence of factor VIII. It presents with prolonged bleeding following tissue injury. Laboratory studies will show a prolonged PTT but normal PT and normal platelet count. Treatment is with recombinant factor VIII.</p><p>Factor VIII deficiency resulting in hemophilia A is the most likely diagnosis in this patient with uncontrolled bleeding from his circumcision site, large right cephalohematoma, an abnormally prolonged partial thromboplastin time (PTT), normal prothrombin time (PT), and normal platelet count. Hemophilia A is an X-linked recessive condition and is therefore more commonly seen in males, while most females are silent carriers. It is caused by an absence or deficiency of factor VIII, which is a component of the intrinsic clotting cascade. Factor VIII is required as a cofactor for the activation of factor X to factor Xa, which in turn converts prothrombin to thrombin to complete the process of clot formation. The PTT measures the activity of the intrinsic pathway and will be prolonged in cases of hemophilia A, while the PT measures the activity of factor VII (extrinsic pathway), which is normal in patients with this disease.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Platelet dysfunction (Choice B) can be inherited or acquired and can also result in prolonged bleeding, but the PTT is usually normal, while bleeding time would be increased. Examples of inherited platelet disorders include Glanzmann thrombasthenia, Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome, and platelet granule disorders.</p><p>Sepsis (Choice C) is an unlikely diagnosis in this patient without fever or signs of infection. Common infections that cause sepsis in the newborn period include meningitis and pneumonia.</p><p>Vitamin K deficiency (Choice D) is unlikely because this child was given a dose of vitamin K, which is adequate to correct any deficiency. Additionally, this would present with a prolonged PT.</p><p>von Willebrand factor deficiency (Choice E) can present with bleeding and is caused by quantitative or qualitative abnormality of von Willebrand factor (vWF), which binds platelets and subendothelial collagen in primary hemostasis. Impaired platelet adherence leads to prolonged bleeding time. It can present with epistaxis, gingival bleeding, petechiae, easy bruising, and menorrhagia.</p>",
"40": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Transient tachypnea of the newborn presents with tachypnea, respiratory distress, cyanosis, and hypoxia caused by a delay in the resorption of amniotic fluid in the alveoli. The fluid leads to decreased compliance and air trapping. Risk factors include preterm delivery, maternal diabetes or asthma, and cesarean delivery. Chest x-ray will show a flattened diaphragm and pulmonary vascular congestion with fluid in the interlobular fissures.</p><p>Transient tachypnea of the newborn presents within a few hours of birth with tachypnea (respirations greater than 60/min), and signs of respiratory distress, such as cyanosis, nasal flaring, and intercostal or subcostal retractions. It is caused by a delay in the resorption of fetal amniotic fluid in the alveoli. The residual fluid in the lung decreases the pulmonary compliance, which leads to an increased work of breathing, tachypnea, and hypoxia in the newborn, and to air trapping through relative compression the bronchioles. Auscultation of the chest is typically normal. Chest x-ray will show a flattened diaphragm and pulmonary congestion with fluid in the interlobular fissures, as well as occasional pleural effusions. Risk factors for its development include preterm birth, diabetes in the mother, cesarean delivery, and maternal asthma. Treatment is typically supportive with supplemental oxygen as needed, with the majority of cases resolving in 24 to 48 hours.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, E, and F.</p><p>Aspiration pneumonia (Choice A) occurs because of the inhalation of orogastric contents into the airway, and most commonly occurs due to dysfunction of the swallowing mechanisms or an inability to protect the airway. Meconium aspiration can occur in the newborn, which can lead to airway obstruction, inflammation, and infection. However, this patient's amniotic fluid was clear, making meconium aspiration unlikely.</p><p>Bacterial pneumonia (Choice B) in the newborn can be caused by infected amniotic fluid, maternal infection, or aspiration of meconium. It commonly presents with fever or hypothermia, respiratory distress, tachycardia, and a chest x-ray with an area of consolidation. This patient is afebrile, and his chest x-ray shows pulmonary edema, rather than a new infiltrate.</p><p>Bronchopulmonary dysplasia (Choice C) is a chronic lung disease that occurs in preterm infants, often requiring prolonged oxygen supplementation. It is caused by alveolar hypoplasia with increased wall thickening, as well as abnormalities of pulmonary vasculature that lead to increased pulmonary resistance. This patient was born at term, making this unlikely.</p><p>Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (Choice D) typically presents in older patients with exertional dyspnea and a chronic cough. Physical examination may disclose inspiratory crackles, and chest imaging will show honeycombing and traction bronchiectasis. It would be unlikely to present in a newborn.</p><p>Pulmonary hemorrhage (Choice E) typically presents with dyspnea, hypoxia, tachycardia, and profound hemoptysis, which may be life-threatening. In children, it can occur with foreign body aspiration, trauma, and bronchiectasis, as well as rheumatologic and hematologic disorders. This patient has no reported history of hemoptysis.</p><p>Respiratory distress syndrome of the newborn (Choice F) occurs in preterm infants because of surfactant deficiency that leads to alveolar collapse. This leads to increased work of breathing, hypoxemia, and cyanosis. It is typically apparent at birth and can be differentiated from transient tachypnea of the newborn with a chest x-ray that shows bilateral ground glass opacities with air bronchograms.</p>",
"41": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: PVR normally decreases immediately after birth to allow for deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to enter the pulmonary vasculature where it is oxygenated. In instances of perinatal asphyxia, the normal decrease in PVR is disrupted leading to persistently increased PVR, otherwise known as pulmonary hypertension. This restricts the blood flow through the pulmonary vasculature and can cause persistent hypoxemia despite adequate oxygen delivery and ventilation.</p><p>Failure of pulmonary vasodilation resulting in significant pulmonary hypertension is the most likely cause of this patient's right-to-left shunt across the foramen ovale and persistent hypoxemia. Normal fetal circulation is unique in that oxygenated blood arrives from the maternal circulation via the umbilical vein and reaches the inferior vena cava directly. From there, oxygenated blood is shunted from the right atrium to the left atrium, to the left ventricle (LV), and then to the systemic circulation. Oxygenated blood that reaches the right ventricle (RV) and the pulmonary arteries is shunted through the ductus arteriosus (DA) to the aortic arch. Before birth the fetal lungs are filled with amniotic fluid and the pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) is high. Under normal circumstances, the first breath leads to a decrease in PVR as pulmonary vessels dilate. Peripartum asphyxia, as in this case, in addition to acidosis, impairs the normal transition from an increased to decreased PVR state. Despite adequate delivery of oxygen after intubation, a persistently increased PVR impairs oxygenation. As such, pulmonary hypertension most likely explains this patient's persistent hypoxemia. Meconium aspiration also causes ventilation-perfusion mismatch, but it is the result of an obstructed bronchial tree with normal perfusion.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, E, and F.</p><p>Closure of the ductus arteriosus (Choice A), which is a shunt between the pulmonary arteries and the aortic arch, typically occurs within several days after birth. Failure to close results in an extracardiac left-to-right shunt and classically presents with a continuous machine-like murmur.</p><p>Closure of foramen ovale (Choice B) occurs within the first 6 months of life and is a normal occurrence. Persistence is known as a patent foramen ovale and causes left-to-right shunting. If large enough, this can eventually result in right-sided hypertrophy and heart failure with shunt reversal.</p><p>Decreased pulmonary vascular resistance (Choice C) is incorrect, as this is a normal occurrence after birth. The decrease in PVR improves oxygenation.</p><p>Increased peripheral vascular resistance (Choice E) causes systemic hypertension but would not affect oxygenation as it has no effect on ventilation or perfusion of the lungs.</p><p>Intracardiac left-to-right shunt (Choice F) can occur as a result of a patent foramen ovale or a ventricular septal defect, which allows oxygenated blood to re-enter the pulmonary vasculature. This would not result in hypoxemia.</p>",
"42": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is a genetic condition characterized by the triad of colonic polyps, mucocutaneous hyperpigmented macules, and an increased risk for gastrointestinal and non-gastrointestinal malignancy. At the time of diagnosis, upper endoscopy and colonoscopy should be performed to evaluate existing polyps and screen for malignancy.</p><p>Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is a genetic condition inherited in an autosomal dominant fashion. It is characterized by the triad of colonic polyps, mucocutaneous hyperpigmented macules, and an increased risk for gastrointestinal and non-gastrointestinal malignancy. In this syndrome, hamartomatous polyps are scattered throughout the gastrointestinal tract and are associated with an increased risk for colorectal and gastric carcinoma. Acute bleeding episodes from one of these polyps leading to bright red blood in the stool, as in this case, are common, but bleeding may also be occult and cause an iron deficiency anemia before it is recognized. Yearly complete blood count to screen for anemia is recommended. Baseline endoscopic evaluation utilizing both upper endoscopy and colonoscopy is recommended to evaluate for malignancy. Blue-gray pigmented macules are scattered primarily in the perioral region and on the lips. They may be confused for freckles but are caused by increased numbers of dermal macrophages containing melanin rather than increased size of melanosomes. The blue-gray cutaneous macules in Peutz-Jeghers syndrome are not malignant.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</p><p>Stool culture (Choice A) is an appropriate next step in the work up of bloody diarrhea thought to be caused by a bacterial infection such as Escherichia coli or Shigella. However, this patient has had blood in her stools for the past 4 months. She also has no fever or signs of infection.</p><p>Air-contrast enema (Choice B) is both diagnostic and therapeutic for intussusception. Intussusception occurs when a segment of bowel telescopes into an adjacent segment, resulting in colicky abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and potential bloody, mucoid stools. Classically, it presents in an infant or young child with recurrent episodes of severe abdominal pain often punctuated by asymptomatic periods.</p><p>Upper gastrointestinal series (Choice C) will not be useful in evaluating colonic polyps. Direct visualization of the colon via colonoscopy is better suited for evaluation of polyps. Furthermore, if a source of bleeding is identified on colonoscopy, intervention can be made to stop it.</p><p>Meckel scan (Choice D), or technetium 99m pertechnetate scan, is the modality used to confirm a Meckel diverticulum, in which case it demonstrates uptake in the right lower quadrant, corresponding with ectopic gastric mucosa within the Meckel diverticulum. A Meckel diverticulum results from persistence of the vitelline duct and can demonstrate ectopic, functional gastric or pancreatic tissue. It can present with acute or chronic hematochezia or melena, often painless, which can lead to the development of iron deficiency anemia.</p>",
"43": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The initial management of an obtunded patient showing signs of respiratory distress should include airway protection, including endotracheal intubation to support the patient's ventilation and oxygenation.</p><p>The patient presents after drinking a large amount of alcohol with altered mental status, obtundation, respiratory distress, and hypoxia. The patient also has signs of chronic excessive alcohol abuse including an enlarged liver palpated below the costal margin and scleral icterus, consistent with alcoholic hepatitis. The patient's obtunded mental status is also associated with disordered breathing, tachypnea, respiratory distress, and hypoxia. The initial management of this patient should include airway protection, including endotracheal intubation to support the patient's ventilation and oxygenation.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Determining the exact amount and type of alcohol ingested (Choice A) and measurement of blood alcohol concentration (Choice B) may be helpful in the care of this patient, but would not be necessary in the emergency management of the patient with disordered breathing and oxygenation. Further elucidating the amount of alcohol ingested may be helpful to determine the time it may take for the patient to metabolize the alcohol.</p><p>Nebulized acetylcysteine therapy (Choice C) is a mucolytic agent that has been used in the treatment of asthma, emphysema, bronchitis, cystic fibrosis, and diseases that may be complicated by mucous plugging of airways. It would not be indicated in this patient, as the patient's respiratory distress and hypoxia is more likely caused by alcohol intoxication.</p><p>Hemodialysis (Choice E) is typically not used in the treatment of alcohol intoxication as the liver is usually able to metabolize alcohol. Dialysis may be helpful in patients with ethylene glycol poisoning and metabolic acidosis.</p>",
"44": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: SLE is an autoimmune connective tissue disease, one manifestation of which is lupus nephritis. Hematuria and proteinuria with a rising creatinine are characteristic of lupus nephritis. Renal biopsy is necessary to accurately determine the type and extent of renal involvement to optimize treatment.</p><p>Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune disorder with a wide range of presentations. In order to make a diagnosis of SLE, four of eleven criteria must be present. These include malar rash, photosensitivity, discoid rash, painless oral ulcers, arthritis, serositis (including pleuritis or pericarditis), neurologic disorder (seizures or psychosis), renal impairment, hematologic abnormalities (hemolytic anemia, leukopenia, lymphopenia, or thrombocytopenia), an abnormal antinuclear antibody assay, or lupus-specific autoantibodies (anti-DNA, anti-Smith, antiphospholipid antibody). The patient described has numerous of these criteria suggesting a diagnosis of SLE. Lupus nephritis is a complication of SLE and is common in younger female patients; it presents with hematuria and rising creatinine in SLE. Evaluation of lupus nephritis requires renal biopsy in order to accurately determine the type and extent of renal involvement. This information is vital for optimizing treatment of nephritis. For example, immunosuppressive therapy is warranted for active diffuse or focal proliferative lupus nephritis but not mild mesangial or mesangial proliferative lupus nephritis.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and D.</p><p>X-rays of the knees (Choice A) is a commonly used modality to begin evaluation of knee pain. However, this patient has numerous other symptoms suggestive of a diagnosis of SLE. The arthritis of SLE is non-erosive and non-deforming. It is typically treated with oral nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, antimalarials, or glucocorticoids. Because of this, not only will x-rays of the knee likely be normal, but they will not change the management of this patient's arthritis. Her proteinuria and hematuria are much more concerning and an indication for renal biopsy.</p><p>Echocardiography (Choice B) is used in evaluating pleural and pericardial effusions. This patient does have pleuritic chest pain suggestive of pleuritis, but she is hemodynamically stable and has no abnormalities on her cardiac examination. The more pressing complication of SLE in this case, which requires further diagnostic effort, is lupus nephritis.</p><p>MRI of the brain (Choice C) and cerebral angiography (Choice D) are both imaging modalities that may be used to evaluate a patient with neuropsychiatric symptoms caused by SLE. There is no evidence that this patient has neuropsychiatric symptoms, and thus, this imaging is not currently warranted.</p>",
"45": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is characterized by excessive daytime sleepiness and snoring. Adenotonsillar hypertrophy is a common cause of OSA in children. If present, the first-line treatment of OSA is adenoidectomy and tonsillectomy.</p><p>Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is common in the pediatric population and is characterized by excessive daytime sleepiness and snoring. Risk factors for pediatric OSA include adenotonsillar hypertrophy and obesity. Both lead to a decreased airway size and increased risk for airway obstruction during sleep, which in turn, leads to pulmonary hypertension. In this case, the x-ray and echocardiography findings indicate the presence of cor pulmonale, or right ventricular dysfunction caused by a primary disorder of the pulmonary system. Other complications of untreated OSA include systemic hypertension, left ventricular dysfunction, failure to thrive, and behavioral problems. This patient also demonstrates adenoid and tonsillar hypertrophy on examination. In children with this finding, the first-line treatment of OSA is adenoidectomy and tonsillectomy. In some cases, watchful waiting is appropriate, but only if the OSA is mild and there are no signs of complication. As this patient shows significant cardiovascular complications secondary to OSA, surgical removal of the adenoids and tonsils is necessary.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, and E.</p><p>Digoxin and diuretic therapy (Choice A) is a therapy used in management of congestive heart failure to improve the contractility of the heart and prevent volume overload. While these modalities may be needed as temporizing measures, they are not a long-term solution.</p><p>Pulmonary vasodilator therapy (Choice B) is used in primary pulmonary hypertension to decrease the pressure within the pulmonary vasculature. This patient's pulmonary hypertension stems from her OSA. Dilating the pulmonary vasculature will not solve the underlying cause of the hypertension.</p><p>Pulmonary artery banding (Choice C) is a therapy used in patients with left transposition of the great arteries to train the left ventricle to pump against the systemic circulation after surgical repair of the great arteries is completed. This patient does not require training of the left ventricle but rather decreased pulmonary resistance by treatment of OSA.</p><p>Repair of tricuspid regurgitation (Choice E) is not appropriate in this patient as it does not address the cause underlying the valvular defect. This patient's tricuspid regurgitation is caused by the dilation of the right ventricle and widening of the valvular opening with inadequate coverage by the valve leaflets. After addressing the underlying cause of cardiomegaly, if the tricuspid regurgitation does not improve, surgical repair may be indicated, but it is not currently.</p>",
"46": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Dermatomyositis is an autoimmune connective tissue disease that causes pink papules over the dorsal fingers (Gottron papules), pink to light purple patches over the upper eyelids (heliotrope sign), light pink to lilac colored patches with telangiectasias on the upper back and sun-exposed chest, dilated capillaries with drop out on the proximal nail folds, and proximal muscle weakness.</p><p>Dermatomyositis is an autoimmune connective tissue disease affecting the skin and the proximal muscles. Cutaneous manifestations include pink papules over the dorsal fingers (Gottron papules), pink to light purple patches over the upper eyelids (heliotrope sign), light pink to lilac colored patches with telangiectasias on the upper back and sun-exposed chest, and dilated capillaries with drop out on the proximal nail folds. Juvenile dermatomyositis also commonly demonstrates calcinosis cutis, the deposition of calcium in the skin. An inflammatory infiltrate of the muscle occurs causing damage to the myocytes, leading to release of creatine kinase, an intracellular molecule. This can be used to monitor disease progress in dermatomyositis. Dermatomyositis is also characterized by several autoantibodies. It demonstrates a positive antinuclear antibody and anti-Jo-1. Anti-SRP and anti-Mi-2 antibodies are also commonly seen. Dermatomyositis is less likely to be associated with malignancy in children than it is in adults. Treatment of dermatomyositis involves systemic steroids followed by long-term immunosuppressive therapy with medications such as methotrexate or azathioprine.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, E, F, G, H, and I.</p><p>Eosinophilic granuloma (Choice B) is a form of Langerhans cell histiocytosis which primarily involves the bones. In children, these lytic bone lesions are commonly seen in the skull. Muscle weakness is not present.</p><p>Inflammatory bowel disease (Choice C) comprises both Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis. Both present with bloody diarrhea and abdominal pain. Muscle weakness is not seen.</p><p>Juvenile rheumatoid arthritis (Choice D), or systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis, is characterized by fever, arthritis, an evanescent, pink-colored rash, and lymphadenopathy.</p><p>Mixed connective tissue disease (Choice E) is an autoimmune connective tissue disease with overlapping, non-specific features of autoimmune connective tissue diseases including systemic lupus erythematosus, dermatomyositis, systemic sclerosis, and rheumatoid arthritis. This patient's symptoms are specific to dermatomyositis.</p><p>Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome (Kawasaki disease) (Choice F) is a multisystemic inflammatory disease in children characterized by fever for at least 5 days, bilateral nonexudative conjunctivitis, a polymorphous rash, lymphadenopathy, erythema and edema of the hands and feet, and erythema affecting the mucous membranes with dry, cracked lips.</p><p>Periarteritis nodosa (Choice G), now known as polyarteritis nodosa, is a medium-vessel vasculitis in which the muscular wall of a medium size artery becomes inflamed. Characteristic symptoms include fatigue, weight loss, weakness, fever, arthralgias, and skin ulcers.</p><p>Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) (Choice H) is an autoimmune disease resulting in a constellation of symptoms including fever, rash, photosensitivity, renal disease, and nonerosive arthritis. The rash in SLE is seen over the malar prominences of the cheeks and spares the nasolabial folds. Eyelid involvement is not typical.</p><p>Systemic sclerosis (scleroderma) (Choice I) presents with taut, hardened skin, Raynaud phenomenon, renal failure, telangiectasias, cardiac fibrosis, esophageal dysmotility, and lower gastrointestinal tract fibrosis. Muscle weakness is not a common characteristic of systemic sclerosis.</p>",
"47": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Lyme disease is a tick-borne disease, caused by B. burgdorferi, endemic in the Northeastern United States. Erythema chronicum migrans, an expanding rash with a central ring-like clearing, is often the first manifestation.</p><p>Lyme disease is a tick-borne disease, caused by Borrelia burgdorferi, endemic in the Northeastern United States. In its early stages, it causes erythema chronicum migrans, which is an expanding rash with a central ring-like clearing as shown in this case. It can also be associated with a flu-like illness. In the early stages of Lyme disease, it can be treated with doxycycline, which acts by preventing the attachment of aminoacyl-tRNA to ribosomes. Adverse effects of tetracycline antibiotics include gastrointestinal distress, photosensitivity, and in children, discoloration of teeth and inhibition of bone growth, which makes it contraindicated in pregnancy. Later stages of Lyme disease can be characterized by carditis, atrioventricular heart block, facial nerve palsy (which is often bilateral), migratory arthralgias, and if left untreated, encephalopathy and chronic arthritis. Later stages of Lyme disease that affect the heart or central nervous system are generally treated with parenteral ceftriaxone.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, E, F, G, I, J, K, and L.</p><p>Atopic dermatitis (Choice A), or eczema, is characterized by pruritus, flexural eczema, a history of asthma or hay fever, and dry skin. The rash is initially characterized by erythematous papules and vesicles with exudates and crusting and, with chronic scratching, becomes dry, scaly, and excoriated in a process called lichenification.</p><p>Cellulitis (Choice B), folliculitis (Choice C), and impetigo (Choice F) are all superficial bacterial infections of the skin. Cellulitis presents with a warm, erythematous patch or plaque, folliculitis with follicle-centric pustules on hair-bearing areas, and impetigo with honey-colored crust typically around the mouth or nose in a child. An expanding, targetoid, erythematous patch is not characteristic of these infections.</p><p>Herpes simplex (Choice D) virus typically begins with the formation of vesicles, which lyse and progress to shallow, painful ulcers with an erythematous border. It is often accompanied by fever, chills, and lymphadenopathy.</p><p>Herpes zoster (Choice E) commonly presents as a unilateral, dermatomal erythematous patch with overlying clear to yellow, grouped vesicles. It is caused by varicella-zoster virus, also known as human herpesvirus-3, which is transmitted via respiratory secretions and direct contact with infected skin lesions. It can be associated with neuropathic pain which can both precede and outlast the rash, but this is more common in the elderly than pediatric patients.</p><p>Juvenile melanoma (Choice G) is suggested by a single lesion with asymmetry, irregular appearing borders, variable coloration, a diameter greater than 6 mm, and rapid evolution in characteristics.</p><p>Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome (Kawasaki disease) (Choice I) is a multisystemic inflammatory disease in children characterized by fever for at least five days, bilateral nonexudative conjunctivitis, a polymorphous rash, lymphadenopathy, erythema and edema of the hands and feet, and erythema affecting the mucous membranes with dry, cracked lips.</p><p>Neurofibromatosis (Choice J) is a genetically inherited disorder characterized by a constellation of cutaneous manifestations including neurofibromas, plexiform neurofibromas, cafe-au-lait macules, and axillary freckling, none of which are present in this patient.</p><p>Nevus flammeus (Choice K) is a common red patch present on the forehead or posterior neck at birth. It is a benign capillary malformation which fades or resolves with time.</p><p>Poison ivy dermatitis (Choice L) is characterized by linearly arranged vesicles and eczematous patches. An expanding targetoid lesion is not consistent with this diagnosis.</p>",
"48": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Enterotoxins commonly cause nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and intestinal cramping within 6 hours after the ingestion of food contaminated by S. aureus.</p><p>Staphylococcus aureus is a Gram-positive organism that is a common cause of cutaneous infections including impetigo, cellulitis, folliculitis, and furunculosis. Staphylococci, especially S. aureus, is associated with the secretion of several exotoxins, including enterotoxin, exfoliatoxin, and toxic shock syndrome toxin. Its enterotoxins commonly cause nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and intestinal cramping after ingestion of food contaminated by S. aureus. S. aureus enterotoxin is heat-stable and associated with the consumption of foods that are contaminated, often cream-based foods. Foods that are left at room temperature allow S. aureus to multiply and produce more enterotoxin. Ingestion of S. aureus enterotoxin leads to nausea, vomiting, and abdominal cramping that typically occurs within 6 hours.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Campylobacter jejuni (Choice A), enteroinvasive Escherichia coli (Choice C), nontyphoidal Salmonella species such as Salmonella enteritidis (Choice D), and Shigella sonnei (Choice E) classically present with mucosal inflammation and invasion, leading to a toxic appearance and bloody diarrhea. This patient is presenting with vomiting and abdominal cramping, not bloody diarrhea, making these organisms less likely.</p><p>Clostridium botulinum (Choice B) creates a botulinum toxin which, if ingested, can also cause a foodborne gastroenteritis resulting in nausea, vomiting, and abdominal cramping; it may also result in neurologic symptoms and flaccid paralysis. However, symptoms typically begin between 18 to 36 hours after the toxin is ingested, later than those of S. aureus-induced foodborne illness.</p>",
"49": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: T lymphocytes play an important role in the control of fungal organisms. Cytotoxic CD8+ T lymphocytes, Th1 cells, and Th17 cells all play a role, thus deficiency in T-lymphocyte function increases the risk for both mucocutaneous and invasive fungal infections.</p><p>Decreased T-lymphocyte activity is the most likely cause of this patient's white, bleeding oral lesions and papular erythematous rash in the diaper area with satellite lesions, which are both consistent with Candida albicans infection. There are many species of Candida, including C. albicans, C. glabrata, C. krusei, C. tropicalis, C. parapsilosis, and C. lusitaniae, although C. albicans tends to be the most commonly implicated organism in mucocutaneous infections. C. albicans is a dimorphic fungus that causes a variety of infections in both immunocompetent and immunocompromised patients, including mucocutaneous infections and invasive blood stream infections, or urinary tract infections. It can phenotypically switch from yeast to hyphae based upon the temperature. T lymphocytes play an important role in preventing fungal infections, so a deficiency of T lymphocytes predisposes to fungal infection. Fungal elements are recognized by antigen presenting cells (APC) through interaction with C-lectin receptors. Interactions between APCs and CD8+ T lymphocytes activate these cells for direct cytotoxic killing; they also induce them to release interleukin-17 (IL-17), which promotes T helper 17 (Th17) cell proliferation. These cells produce IL-17 and IL-22, which attract neutrophils, which are also fungicidal. Additional cytokines released during this also induce development of Th1 cells, which have been shown to play a role in fungal immunity.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: A, B, C, E, F, G, and H.</p><p>Decreased B-lymphocyte activity (Choice A) is seen in disorders such as X-linked agammaglobulinemia and results in deficient humoral immunity. These patients are at higher risk for invasive bacterial infections.</p><p>Decreased dendritic cell activity (Choice B) would predispose to infection with bacteria, viruses, and fungi. Dendritic cells are APCs that interact with T and B lymphocytes to present antigens necessary for both cellular and humoral immunity. Alternatively, increased dendritic cell activity (Choice F) would result in activation of both B and T lymphocytes and would not cause an immunodeficiency syndrome, although overactivation may be associated with autoimmune disorders.</p><p>Decreased segmented neutrophil function (Choice C) is seen in a variety of disorders and may include defects in neutrophil chemotaxis or function. Patients present with recurrent bacterial infections. Increased segmented neutrophil function (Choice G) would not cause an immunodeficiency syndrome.</p><p>Increased B-lymphocyte activity (Choice E) is seen in response to bacterial and viral infection as these cells differentiate into memory B lymphocytes and plasma cells to secrete immunoglobulins, which are important for attenuating future infections.</p><p>Increased T-lymphocyte activity (Choice H) is associated with some autoimmune conditions but does not lead to an increased risk for fungal infections.</p>",
"50": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: In Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease, an autosomal dominant mutation expressed in Schwann cells leads to symmetric motor and sensory polyneuropathy that begins in the distal lower extremities and may progress to the upper extremities. Muscle weakness typically results in hyporeflexia and progresses to muscle atrophy. Sensation to vibration and proprioception is frequently affected. Patients demonstrate a clumsy gait and consequent skeletal deformities such as pes cavus and hammer toes.</p><p>Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease (CMT) is a heritable disease of peripheral neuropathy. As a result of primarily autosomal dominant mutations in genes expressed in Schwann cells, the myelinating cells of the peripheral nervous system, peripheral sensory and motor nerves become progressively demyelinated. Patients demonstrate motor weakness and decreased sensation to vibration and proprioception most prominent in the distal lower extremities. Physical examination findings include hyporeflexia or areflexia, along with clumsy walking and consequent musculoskeletal deformities such as pes cavus (high arch of the foot) and hammer toes (toes with an abnormal upward bend of the middle joint). Later in the disease course, atrophy of the lower extremity muscles and intrinsic hand muscle weakness and atrophy may occur. Diagnosis occurs via nerve conduction studies, which may show decreased conduction velocities and prolonged distal motor latencies consistent with demyelinating disease (some disease subtypes show decreased amplitudes suggestive of axonal loss). Treatment is supportive and includes physical therapy.</p><p>Incorrect Answers: B, C, D, and E.</p><p>Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (Choice B) is an acquired, immune-mediated disease of the peripheral nerve roots that leads to symmetric proximal and distal motor weakness and decreased sensation to vibration and proprioception. The course may be relapsing-remitting or progressive. In this patient with a family history suggestive of peripheral neuropathy, CMT is more likely.</p><p>Diabetic neuropathy (Choice C) is an acquired, symmetric polyneuropathy that predominantly affects the sensory nerves of the distal lower and upper extremities. Motor weakness typically occurs only in severe cases, after years of hyperglycemia.</p><p>Duchenne muscular dystrophy (Choice D), caused by an inherited mutation in the dystrophin gene, typically presents at age 2 to 3 years with progressive muscle weakness affecting the proximal more than the distal muscles. Cardiac conduction abnormalities are also common. Sensory neuropathy would be atypical.</p><p>Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy (Choice E) an inherited muscular dystrophy resulting in slowly progressive, asymmetric muscle weakness in the facial, scapular, upper arm, lower leg, and abdominal muscles. Sensory neuropathy would be atypical.</p>"
};

